# ======================================================================
# decl-testers.agg  --  DecL language stress library (shipped)
# ======================================================================
#
# A flat, greppable corpus that exercises DecL *language* coverage rather
# than building interesting aggregates: the feature surface the parser must
# accept (every exposure/severity/frequency form, reinsurance, splicing,
# signed / P&L, bivariate (bv), distortion constructors, hints, and
# intentional parser-error fixtures). The companion `library.agg` is the
# shipped, documented library -- the insurance-useful worked examples and the
# default knowledge base. This file is a corpus, not a curated surface: it
# reuses names across kinds deliberately, so the uniqueness rule that governs
# `library.agg` does not apply here.
#
# KEPT IN SYNC WITH pytest: each program here mirrors one embedded in the
# ``tests/`` tree (the Python module is the canonical source). When you add a
# pytest case with a DecL program, append it here under the matching section.
#
# Letter prefixes P..X continue the A..O of `_test_suite.agg`, so the two do
# not collide if loaded together.
#
# DecL syntax reminders honoured here:
#   * ``port`` definitions are single-line (sub-agg clauses inlined).
#   * the trailer clauses ``note{...}`` / ``tags{...}`` / ``hints{...}`` are
#     legal on every statement, ``dist`` / ``distortion`` included (since
#     1.0.0a157).

# Contents
#
# P. Baseline harness — Aggregate regression cases   (tests/baseline/corpus.py)
# Q. Baseline harness — Portfolio regression cases   (tests/baseline/corpus.py)
# R. PEG regression — two-unit lognorm + gamma mix   (tests/peg.py)
# S. Bounds regression — bounded BDD portfolio       (tests/test_bounds.py)
# T. Splice / layered-severity regressions           (tests/test_splice_suite.py)
# U. Validation regressions — symmetric & low-skew   (tests/test_validation.py)
# V. Underwriter / build smoke fixtures              (tests/test_underwriter.py)
# W. Distortion DecL constructors                    (tests/test_distortion_construction.py)
# X. Parser-error fixtures (intentional typos)       (tests/test_parser_errors_integration.py)


# P. Baseline harness — Aggregate regression cases
# ================================================
# Pinned grids and snapshots in tests/baseline/data/. Each case targets a
# specific path through the FFT pipeline; together they fence the v1.0
# core-compute refactor. See tests/baseline/corpus.py for the grids
# (log2/bs/padding/normalize) bound to each program.
agg P.Base.FixedOne 1 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 0.5 fixed                                                                 note{fixed-1 shortcut path};
agg P.Base.DfreqOne dfreq [1] sev lognorm 100 cv 0.5                                                                     note{full-FFT path equivalent to FixedOne};
agg P.Sym.Dice     dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]                                                                                  note{symmetric discrete; skew dust regression};
agg P.Tail.LN      5 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 3 poisson                                                                 note{thick tail; tail accuracy on wide grid};
agg P.Re.Both    100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 3500 po 4000 xs 1000 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000   note{occurrence + aggregate reinsurance};
agg ReBucket      10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 0.8 po 250 xs 175 poisson                              note{off-grid excess attach; reins_bucket linear/nearest rebucketing};
agg P.Def.Pareto   1 claim sev 1000 * pareto 1.5 - 1000 fixed                                                            note{defective; no finite 2nd moment} hints{normalize=False};
agg P.Mix         [10 20] claims [100 400] xs 0 sev lognorm [20 40 100] cv [.5 .6 .7] wts=3 mixed gamma .2                note{bounded mixture, mixed gamma frequency}


# Q. Baseline harness — Portfolio regression cases
# ================================================
# Three portfolios cover (i) unbounded gamma/lognorm with fixed-1 frequency,
# (ii) bounded beta mixture, (iii) bounded discrete (Bodoff). Used to pin
# linear/lifted allocation and the bounded-detection refusal of
# ``allocation='lifted'`` on unbounded + mass distortions.
port Q.Port.CNC     agg CNC.NonCat 25 claim sev gamma 80 cv 0.15 mixed gamma .2 agg CNC.Cat 5 claim 200 xs 0 sev lognorm 40 cv 1.50 mixed ig .2;
port Q.Port.Bounded agg A dfreq [1:3] sev 500 * beta 5 2 agg B dfreq [1:5] sev 800 * beta 7 2;
port Q.Port.Bodoff  agg wind 1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0, 99] [0.80, 0.20] fixed agg quake 1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0, 100] [0.95, 0.05] fixed


# R. PEG regression — two-unit portfolio
# ======================================
# The calibrate-distortions / pricing-pipeline regression fixture
# (tests/peg.py). Exercises a limit-and-attachment severity, a three-component
# lognorm mixture per unit, and different mixing CVs (0.25 vs 0.35) for the
# mixed-vs-independent correlation case. Five calibrated distortions follow
# (ccoc / ph / wang / dual / tvar). The DecL is the contract — do not change
# without re-capturing ``tests/data/peg_baseline.json``.
port R.PEG agg A 100 claims 50 xs 0 sev lognorm [30 50 100] cv [0.1 .6 1.8] wts [.5 .25 .25] mixed gamma .25 agg B 150 claims 100 xs 0 sev lognorm [30 50 100] cv [0.1 .6 1.8] wts [.5 .25 .25] mixed gamma .35


# S. Bounds regression — BDD portfolio
# ====================================
# Bounded beta severities, fixed-1 frequency: a finite-support portfolio
# where the closed-form anchor (premium = TVaR_0.5(total) → p_star = 0.5)
# lets the ``Bounds`` cloud columns collapse to a known distortion. See
# tests/test_bounds.py.
port S.BDD agg A 1 claim sev 10 * beta 2 3 fixed agg B 1 claim sev 15 * beta 4 2 fixed


# T. Splice / layered-severity regressions
# ========================================
# Pins the analytical moments for spliced + layered compositions
# (tests/test_splice_suite.py). T.Splice01/02 are bare spliced severities;
# T.SpliceLayer01/02 wrap them in layer-and-attachment policies whose
# conditional moments are computable by hand.
sev T.Splice01      lognorm 10 cv .5 splice [20 30]                                           note{single-segment splice; mean and moms finite};
sev T.Splice02 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12]                                                 note{shift+scale splice; fz.sf(9) ~ 0.75 splice-only survival};
agg T.SpliceLayer01 1 claim 5 xs 3 sev 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12] fixed                   note{full-limit; E[claim^k] = 5^k};
agg T.SpliceLayer02 1 claim 2 xs 8 sev 10 * uniform + 5 splice [8 12] fixed                   note{proper excess; E[claim] = 1.5, E[claim^2] = 8/3, E[claim^3] = 5};
agg T.SpliceUnbounded 5 claims sev lognorm 40 cv .65 splice [1 100] poisson                   note{splice on unbounded base; window must be finite}


# U. Validation regressions — symmetric & low-skew
# ================================================
# Symmetric distributions whose analytic skew is exactly 0. The validation
# code must not flag empirical skew dust as a CV/skew failure
# (tests/test_validation.py).
agg U.Die     dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]                                                            note{fair die; analytic skew = 0};
agg U.LN     25 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 0.75 poisson                                         note{genuinely skewed; skew > 0.1 preserved};
port U.TestSym agg A dfreq [1] dsev [1:6] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]


# V. Underwriter / build smoke fixtures
# =====================================
# Single-output / multi-output / kind-dispatch coverage for ``build()`` and
# the knowledge base (tests/test_underwriter.py). PhaseZero: names match
# the pre-refactor pin so renaming is detectable.
agg V.PhaseZero:Dice  dfreq [3] dsev [1:6]                                                    note{build() returns Aggregate};
sev V.PhaseZero:S     lognorm 100 cv 1                                                        note{build() returns Severity};
port V.PhaseZero:P agg A1 1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed agg A2 1 claim sev lognorm 20 cv 1 fixed;
distortion V.PhaseZero:D ph 0.5;  # build() returns Distortion
agg V.PhaseZero:IP    1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed                                       note{_interpret_program: knowledge filled, object None};
# Multi-output: build() raises ValueError, build_many returns the list.
agg V.PhaseZero:Multi1 1 claim sev lognorm 10 cv 1 fixed;
agg V.PhaseZero:Multi2 1 claim sev lognorm 20 cv 1 fixed;
# discover()/describe()/plot() smoke fixtures — exercise the kind-dispatch
# in Underwriter.discover for sev and distortion.
sev        V.DiscSevTest   lognorm 100 cv 1;
distortion V.DiscDistTest  ph 0.3;
sev        V.DiscPlotSev   lognorm 100 cv 1;
distortion V.DiscPlotDist  ph 0.3


# W. Distortion DecL constructors
# ===============================
# Combo distortions (minimum, mixture) reference child distortions by name
# via ``dist.X`` / ``distortion.X`` (tests/test_distortion_construction.py).
# Both prefixes resolve through the knowledge base.
#
# Leaf distortions are live and validated by interpret_file. The four combo
# lines below are commented out because interpret_file() parses without
# registering what it parses, so a reference to an entry declared earlier in
# the same file does not resolve. Combo lookups need the children already in
# the recipe base, which only a sequential file load (Underwriter.load) gives
# them. They are listed verbatim for reference; uncomment when loading via
# Underwriter.load.
dist W.tcc       ccoc .25;
dist W.tdu       dual 2.5;
dist W.tph       ph   0.7;
# Multi-parameter kinds take a FLAT number list (no brackets); the kind ->
# parameter-name mapping lives on the Distortion subclass (decl_params), applied
# by Distortion.decl_spec. See tests/test_distortion_decl.py.
dist W.tbi       bitvar 0.9 0.99 0.5;
dist W.tpw       power 0.01 1.0 2;
dist W.tbe       beta 0.5 2
# dist W.tMIN      minimum dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu                            # MinimumDistortion of two
# dist W.tMIX      mixture dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu wts [0.6 0.4]              # explicit weights
# dist W.tMIX3     mixture dist.W.tcc dist.W.tdu dist.W.tph                 # uniform default weights (3 children)
# dist W.tMIN.full minimum distortion.W.tcc distortion.W.tdu                # full ``distortion.`` prefix also works


# X. Parser-error fixtures (intentional typos)
# ============================================
# These lines DO NOT parse — they exercise the error-report pipeline
# (tests/test_parser_errors_integration.py, tests/test_parser_errors.py).
# Excluded from the read-database loaders (they would log a parse error).
# Listed for reference only; commented so test_suite parsers skip them.
#
# agg X.Typo01 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 mixd poisson 0.5     note{"mixd" -> "mixed"; UnexpectedCharacters; suggestion in report}
# agg X.Typo02 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 + +                  note{stray punctuation; UnexpectedToken}
# agg X.Typo03 100 claims sev lognorm                               note{truncated; UnexpectedEOF}
# agg X.Typo04 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 ?                    note{unknown character; no suggestions}


# Y. Reinsurance reporting regressions
# ====================================
# Drive the rationalized reins reporting surface (1.0.0a19): the consistent-
# column reins_density_df, the per-stage/per-view/per-basis reins_stats_df
# (EX exact vs Est rebucketed), and the per-stage reins_summary_df (occurrence
# leads Gross, aggregate leads Subject). See tests/test_reins_reporting.py and
# dev/reins-reporting.md.
#
# Structural cases (consistent columns, mass, describe shape) on lognormal
# books -- one per stage combination.
agg RR.Occ  10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 0.8 po 250 xs 175 poisson                       note{occurrence only; off-grid excess attach};
agg RR.Agg  10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 500 xs 750                                note{aggregate only};
agg RR.Both 100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 3500 po 4000 xs 1000 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000   note{occurrence + aggregate};
# Bounded severities for the EX-vs-Est numeric checks: a bounded aggregate has
# a negligible FFT deficit, so linear preserves the rebucketed mean exactly and
# nearest stays within bs/2. Fractional-share off-grid cessions exercise the
# rebucketing scatter.
agg RR.OccB 10 claims sev 500 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.8 po 150 xs 100 poisson                          note{bounded; off-grid occ cession};
agg RR.AggB 10 claims sev 200 * beta 2 3 poisson aggregate net of 0.7 po 300 xs 400                           note{bounded; off-grid agg cession};
# Portfolio end-to-end gcn: a ceding port (occ on A, agg on B, gross C) and a
# gross-only control (reins objects return None).
port RR.Port agg A 100 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 2000 xs 1000 poisson agg B 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 2000 agg C 20 claims sev lognorm 30 cv 1 poisson;
port RR.Gross agg A 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg B 5 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson


# Z. Bivariate (joint ceded, net) occurrence reinsurance
# ======================================================
# Drive Aggregate.occ_bivariate (1.0.0a20): the joint law of aggregate
# occurrence ceded C and net N via 2D FFT. Each program carries occurrence
# reinsurance; the bivariate marginals reproduce the reins_density_df occ
# ceded/net aggregates and the anti-diagonal reproduces the gross aggregate.
# See tests/test_reins_bivariate.py and dev/done/reins-bivariate.md.
agg BV.Occ  10 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 50 xs 50 poisson                               note{lognormal; general deficit-carrying path};
agg BV.OccB 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson                            note{bounded; tightest marginal match; positive corr};
agg BV.Fix  dfreq [4] dsev [1:20] occurrence net of 6 xs 6                                                    note{fixed count; empirical-freq PGF on the 2D grid};
agg BV.Ced  8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence ceded to 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson                          note{ceded-to output view; bivariate ignores the view}


# P&L. Negative-support (signed) severity and the output window (1.0.0a21)
# =======================================================================
# Drive negative-x: a profit is a negative loss. dsev with a negative atom
# auto-signs; continuous P&L severities use the ssev keyword (the never-clamp
# sibling of sev). A signed aggregate auto-windows from build/update (x_min
# defaults to 'auto' -> a two-sided window); a number forces the origin. See
# tests/test_negative_x.py and dev/plan-negative-x-agg.md.
agg PnL.Fix  dfreq [3] dsev [-2 5] [.5 .5]                                                                   note{fixed-3; closed form support (-6,1,8,15)};
agg PnL.SD   dfreq [3] dsev [-1 1]                                                                           note{zero-mean signed; sev SD 1, agg SD sqrt(3) -- derived from ex2, not mean*cv};
agg PnL.Sym  20 claims dsev [-1 1] [.5 .5] poisson                                                           note{symmetric P&L; mean 0};
agg PnL.Lump 1e6 claims dsev [-1 10] [15/16 1/16] poisson                                                    note{near-Gaussian lump at a negative mean ~ -312500; auto window};
agg PnL.Norm 100 claims ssev 50 * norm + 10 poisson                                                          note{continuous signed (ssev) P&L severity; never clamps};
agg PnL.PmL  5 claims ssev 100 - lognorm 80 cv .2 poisson                                                    note{premium minus loss: constant - dist reflects the severity (sev mean 20)};
agg PnL.SLay 5 claims 25000 xs 0 ssev 100 - lognorm 80 cv .2 poisson                                         note{layer clause on a signed severity: parses and round-trips, but the build drops it with an IgnoredDecLClauseWarning};
agg PnL.DLay 1 claim 3 xs 0 dsev [-2 5] [.6 .4] fixed                                                        note{same on a dsev that auto-signs; the dropped clause restores limit to the atom support max 5}


# Orient. The payoff/loss orientation suffix on an agg (1.0.0a102)
# ============================================================
# A trailing payoff/loss keyword sets the variable's sign-convention role
# (value_type) WITHOUT any reflect/shift -- pure orientation, distinct from the
# pnl affine wrapper. payoff marks an asset-return / direct-payoff primitive
# ("more is better") that prices through the dual distortion; loss is the
# explicit default. Placed last, before the note/hints trailer (after
# freq/reins/approximate). See tests/test_negative_x.py and dev/plan-pnl.md S4.
agg Orient.Payoff 100 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson payoff                                              note{asset-return primitive; prices through the dual};
agg Orient.Loss   100 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson loss                                                note{explicit loss default};
agg Orient.Dfreq  dfreq [1 2 3] dsev [1 2 3] payoff                                                          note{dfreq form carries the suffix too}


# PortPnL. Portfolio combine on signed (P&L) support (1.0.0a22)
# ============================================================
# Independent signed units combine onto a shared signed grid: the FFT product
# is origin-at-0 (ftagg_density is independent of each unit's x_min) and a
# single F2 roll places the total on [x_min_tot, ...). Comparable-scale units
# share one bs so the combine is exact (means add, variances add). A signed +
# ordinary mix straddles 0. Pricing columns (add_exa) are deferred -> F/S only.
# See tests/test_negative_x_port.py and dev/plan-negative-x-port.md.
port PortPnL.Pair  agg A 50 claims ssev 10 * norm + 5 poisson agg B 50 claims ssev 10 * norm - 5 poisson;
port PortPnL.Mixed agg Loss 30 claims sev lognorm 20 cv 0.8 poisson agg PnL 50 claims ssev 10 * norm poisson

# ============================================================
# PnLprem. The pnl keyword: premium-minus-loss aggregates (1.0.0a23)
# ============================================================
# pnl NAME <premium> prem less <loss body> builds an ordinary loss aggregate and
# applies an aggregate-level affine wrapper PnL = premium less A (reflect + a
# single shift by the total premium). The premium is subtracted ONCE for the
# book (unlike a constant inside sev/ssev, which is per-claim). value_type is
# set to payoff. Three exposure heads: N% lr (binds to the premium), N claims,
# N loss. The premium vectorises like an agg exposure (total pnl only). A pnl
# line goes anywhere an agg does, including as a port unit. See tests/test_pnl.py
# and dev/done/plan-pnl-premium.md.
pnl PnLprem.LR     1000 prem less agg PnLprem.LR_e 1000 prem at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                         note{loss ratio form: E[loss]=700, margin 300};
pnl PnLprem.Claims 100 prem less agg PnLprem.Claims_e 10 claims sev lognorm 8 cv 1.5 poisson                                       note{claims form: premium independent of the loss model};
pnl PnLprem.Loss   100 prem less agg PnLprem.Loss_e 85 loss sev lognorm 8 cv 1.5 poisson                                         note{expected-loss form: E[loss]=85, margin 15};
pnl PnLprem.Vec    [100 200 100] prem less agg PnLprem.Vec_e [100 200 100] prem at .8 lr [1000 2000 5000] xs 0 sev lognorm 500 cv 2 poisson           note{vector consideration sums to 400; mean 80 = sum premium_i (1-lr)};
# Engine-source forms (1.0.0a125): a pnl / xpnl wraps a COMPLETE engine.
# ``inherit premium`` copies the engine's technical premium; ``xpnl`` returns
# the multi-group step walk (a136). The stored-``agg.NAME`` / ``port.NAME``
# source forms need a loaded referent, so they live in
# tests/test_pnl_engine_source.py (this corpus round-trips each line in
# isolation). See dev/done/plan-pnl-engine-source.md.
pnl  ES.Inherit inherit premium less agg ES.Inherit_e 8000 prem at 65% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson            note{inherit copies the engine's 8000 technical premium; margin 8000 - 5200};
xpnl ES.Tower   1000 prem less agg ES.Tower_e 1000 prem at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence ceded to 500 xs 500 deposit 100 poisson  note{the xpnl walk: gross -> occ cover -> Total, marginal-stitched};
# [PnL-Generic-Final] (dev/plan-yapnl.md example programs, 1.0.0a129-a131): a
# pnl is a source plus a signed GROUP LEDGER -- cash-flow-parts rows, EX adds
# down the sheet. The acceptance pair (YAPNL.Gross vs YAPNL.Retro) shares one
# exhibit template: a feature never changes the machinery, it changes one
# leg's function. See tests/test_variable_rating_decl.py and tests/test_pnl*.py.
pnl YAPNL.Gross as "Gross Book PNL" inherit premium as "Gross Premium" less agg YAPNL.Gross_e as "Gross Loss" 10000 premium at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson less 5% loss expense as LAE 100 fixed expense and 10% premium expense as "Fixed & Acq Exp"  note{acceptance pair 1: plain gross book ledger, LAE stochastic};
pnl YAPNL.Retro as "Retro Rated Gross PNL" retro basic 2000 lcm 1.1 min 8000 max 14000 premium as "Retro Premium" less agg YAPNL.Retro_e as "Gross Loss" 10000 premium at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson less 5% loss expense as LAE 100 fixed expense as "Fixed Exp" 10% premium expense as "Acq Exp"  note{acceptance pair 2: same shape, stochastic retro premium};
pnl YAPNL.AggCeded 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.AggCeded_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 deposit 500 cede 10% less 20% premium expense  note{consolidated pnl: net premium vs net loss; the two-group walk is the xpnl face (a136)};
pnl YAPNL.OccReinst 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.OccReinst_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 occurrence net of 24500 xs 500 deposit 1000 reinstatements [1] mixed gamma 0.25 less 20% premium expense  note{2-D ledger over the (L, R) joint; ceded premium D + h(R) stochastic};
pnl YAPNL.Swing 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.Swing_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 swing basic 0 lcm 1.1 min 300 max 1000 less 20% premium expense  note{consolidated: stochastic swing ceded premium folds into the net premium leg};
pnl YAPNL.Slide 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.Slide_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 deposit 1500 slide 45% at 60% and 25% at 70% and 19% at 80% less 20% premium expense  note{consolidated: sliding commission credit rides the net premium leg};
pnl YAPNL.PC 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.PC_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 deposit 1500 pc 50% after 10% less 20% premium expense  note{consolidated: profit commission credit rides the net premium leg};
pnl YAPNL.Corridor 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.Corridor_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 deposit 500 corridor 50% po 30% xs 20% less 20% premium expense  note{consolidated: corridor-adjusted recovery shapes the net loss leg};
xpnl YAPNL.Onion 10000 premium less agg YAPNL.Onion_e 10000 premium at 75% lr 10000 xs 0 sev 10.808 * lognorm 1.75 occurrence net of 24500 xs 500 deposit 1000 mixed gamma 0.25 aggregate net of 5000 xs 10000 deposit 200 less 20% premium expense  note{the guaranteed-cost walk: gross -> occ -> agg -> Total, every row an affine of a reins_density_df marginal};
# (Portfolios of pnl units -- book-level P&L -- are deferred; a pnl is the
# standalone PnL veneer, not a portfolio unit. See dev/plan-pnl.md.)
# Signed loss severity (dsev negative atom / ssev) under pnl (1.0.0a45): the
# loss convolves on its genuine signed grid; the net is C - loss on that grid.
# See tests/test_pnl.py and dev/done/plan-pnl-signed-severity.md.
pnl PnLsigned.Dsev   5 premium less agg PnLsigned.Dsev_e dfreq[3] dsev[-1 1]                                                        note{signed loss: PnL in (2,4,6,8), mean 5, sd sqrt3};
pnl PnLsigned.Asym   10 premium less agg PnLsigned.Asym_e dfreq[2] dsev[-2 1 3] [.5 .3 .2]                                          note{non-symmetric signed dsev: mean = 10 - 2 E[X] = 10.2};
pnl PnLsigned.Ssev   100 premium less agg PnLsigned.Ssev_e 5 claims ssev 20 - lognorm 10 cv 0.5 poisson                            note{continuous signed severity: E[loss]=50, margin 50}

# ============================================================
# MV. The bivariate keyword: copula-coupled aggregates (1.0.0a24)
# ============================================================
# bivariate NAME <shared-count> <two agg/pnl components> copula KIND P [freq]
# couples the two components' per-claim severities by a copula and accumulates
# them with a shared outer frequency via a 2D FFT. Each component's dfreq [0 1]
# [p0 p1] is the per-event trigger; its aggregate is the per-event severity
# g_i = (1-p_i) delta_0 + p_i f_i. Marginalising one axis reproduces that
# component's standalone aggregate. A pnl component contributes its loss
# severity and applies the premium as a per-axis affine after the FFT (the
# loss-loss copula dependence becomes profit-loss). Copulas (aggregate.copula):
# normal (Pearson rho), gumbel (Kendall tau), clayton (Kendall tau), fgm
# (Spearman rho_s), independent; t is deferred. See tests/test_bivariate.py
# and dev/plan-mv.md.
# Programs are one-per-line (DecL is whitespace-insensitive within a program).
bivariate MV.Gumbel 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson;
bivariate MV.ClaytonMixed 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula clayton 0.3 mixed gamma .2;
bivariate MV.Normal 25 claims agg A dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 agg B dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev gamma 50 cv 1.0 copula normal 0.5;
bivariate MV.Indep 25 claims agg A dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 agg B dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev gamma 50 cv 1.0 poisson;
# netceded MODE: the joint occurrence law of one reinsured aggregate (same object
# as Aggregate.occ_bivariate()). Body is an ordinary agg carrying occurrence
# reinsurance. The three view-pair prefixes name the (x, y) axes x-then-y:
# netceded -> (Net, Ceded); grossceded -> (Gross, Ceded); grossnet -> (Gross, Net).
netceded agg MV.NetCeded 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson;
grossceded agg MV.GrossCeded 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson;
grossnet agg MV.GrossNet 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson;
# CLASH statement: shared-event clash model. (na, nb, nc) = A-only / B-only /
# both; the solver derives the shared count n and the per-event triggers, so the
# two components are independent-copula Bernoulli factories on a shared frequency.
clash MV.Clash 8 5 2 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.2 sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 mixed gamma 0.2

# DBVSEV. Discrete bivariate severity: the joint per-claim probability matrix
# given directly on an explicit lattice (mode='discrete'), the 2-D analogue of
# dsev. Row index = X outcome, column index = Y outcome; matrix[i][j] =
# P(X=xs[i], Y=ys[j]). Four forms: dfreq + dbvsev (headline), exposure-count +
# dbvsev + freq, dfreq + agg/agg copula, and the sparse-triples / uniform-default
# / range variants. Marginals reproduce the standalone dsev compounds exactly.
# See dev/done/plan-bv-discrete.md.
bivariate MV.DBV4 dfreq [1 2 3] [.5 .3 .2] dbvsev [0 1 2] [0 5 10] [[.4 .1 .0] [.1 .1 .1] [.0 .05 .15]];
bivariate MV.DBV3 5 claims dbvsev [10 20] [1 2 3] [[.4 .1 .0] [.2 .1 .2]] mixed gamma 0.1;
bivariate MV.DBVPoi 5 claims dbvsev [10 20] [1 2 3] [[.4 .1 .0] [.2 .1 .2]];
bivariate MV.DBVSparse 5 claims dbvsev [[10 1 .4] [10 2 .1] [20 1 .2] [20 2 .1] [20 3 .2]];
bivariate MV.DBVUniform 5 claims dbvsev [0 1 2] [0 5 10];
bivariate MV.DBVRange 5 claims dbvsev [0:2] [0:2];
bivariate MV.DBVIndep dfreq [1] dbvsev [0 1 2] [0 1 2] [[.30 .15 .05] [.18 .09 .03] [.12 .06 .02]];
bivariate MV.DBV2 dfreq [1 2] [.7 .3] agg A dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 50 cv 1 agg B dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev gamma 50 cv 1.0 copula gumbel 0.4

# ============================================================
# HINTS. note{} is pure text; hints{} carries key=value; build settings (1.0.0a25)
# ============================================================
# `note{...}` is free-text annotation only; `hints{...}` carries build/update
# settings (`key=value;`) parsed by the underwriter under caller-wins rules
# (explicit build() kwargs override in-program hints). note{} and hints{} are
# both optional and order-free, at most one of each. Unknown hint keys warn and
# are dropped; a duplicate key warns and the last value wins; `=`-bearing prose
# in a note no longer crashes the build. See tests/test_hints.py.
agg HINT.LogTwo  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{log2=12};
agg HINT.Frac    1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.1 fixed hints{bs=1/64; log2=10};
agg HINT.Both    5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson note{just prose} hints{log2=12};
agg HINT.HintsFirst 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{log2=12} note{just prose};
agg HINT.ProseEq dfreq [3] dsev [-2 5] [.5 .5] note{closed form support -6,1,8,15; needs x_min<=-6};
agg HINT.Unknown 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson hints{frobnicate=3};
agg HINT.Dup     1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.1 fixed hints{bs=1; bs=2}

# `format_program` / `obj.format_program` render these WITHOUT the trailer:
# that is the default since 1.0.0a166 (formatting a program is about the math
# and the insurance, not the metadata around it). One flag governs all three
# clauses -- note{}, tags{}, hints{} -- since they are one grammar
# construct; `trailer=True` brings them all back and an iterable such as
# `trailer=('hints',)` keeps only what it names. The suppressed form is NOT
# round-trip safe: it re-parses to the same spec with the trailer blanked.
# The semantic `!` (here the zero-modified mean pin) is clause syntax and
# always survives. A portfolio follows the same rule for its units, not just
# the head line. In spread layout each surviving clause takes its own line.
# See tests/test_fcc_surface.py, tests/test_doc_clause.py.
agg FCC.Note 10 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson note{a stored note} hints{log2=16};
agg FCC.RT   10 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson note{hello} hints{log2=16};
agg FCC.Pin  10 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson zm 0.5 note{drop me};
port FCC.NoteP
	agg N1 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson note{unit one}
	agg N2 3 claims sev gamma 5 cv .5 poisson note{unit two}

# ============================================================
# DISC. Discrete severity backed by an honest frozen discrete RV (1.0.0a26)
# ============================================================
# `dsev`/`fixed` severities now use `_DiscreteRV` (exact step cdf/sf, pdf=0,
# exact ppf/isf/support) instead of the old rv_histogram epsilon-jump hack.
# Every discrete moment -- unlimited, limited, or layered -- is an exact finite
# sum over the atoms, so a discrete severity never routes its moments through
# numerical isf-integration. See tests/test_discrete_severity.py.
agg DISC.Dice  dfreq [1] dsev [1:6];
agg DISC.Layer 3 claims 5 xs 3 dsev [1 4 8] [.5 .3 .2] fixed;
# Off-grid atoms: with a non-integer bs these miss the grid, so the
# `dsev_bucket` placement scheme matters. `dsev_bucket='linear'` (default,
# a build/update kwarg -- not DecL syntax) splits each atom's mass across its
# two bracketing buckets to preserve the mean exactly; `'nearest'` snaps and
# biases by <= bs/2. See tests/test_dsev_bucket.py.
agg DISC.OffGrid dfreq [1] dsev [0.3 1.7 2.4] [.5 .3 .2]

# ============================================================
# TAIL. Ordered tail-thickness classification (1.0.0a29)
# ============================================================
# Deterministic family lookup gives each aggregate an ordered tail rung
# (bounded < super-exponential < exponential < subexponential < power-law) and
# a log-concave flag, surfaced via `.tail_class`, `.tail_description`,
# `.tail_explanation`, and `.info`. `.bounded` is now derived (bounded iff the
# aggregate tail class is BOUNDED). The aggregate rung is `max(freq, sev)`.
# See tests/test_tail.py and src/aggregate/tail.py (Phase 1; numeric estimator
# for histogram/meta/spliced families is deferred to Phase 2).
agg TAIL.Subexp   100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson;
agg TAIL.Expon    100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson;
agg TAIL.PowerLaw 100 claims sev 100 * pareto 3 poisson;
agg TAIL.FreqDriven 100 claims sev 100 * uniform poisson;
agg TAIL.Bounded  dfreq [2] dsev [1 2 3];
agg TAIL.Mixed    10 claims sev [100 200] * [lognorm pareto] [2 3] wts [.5 .5] poisson

# ============================================================
# GH. Exponential-tilting pedagogy (Grübel-Hermesmeier, 1.0.0a46)
# ============================================================
# The Poisson/Levy aliasing demo reproduced by
# aggregate.pedagogy.gh_tilting_exhibit / tilted_aggregate_density. Tilting is a
# teaching device only; the production convolution is tilt-free (padding is the
# operational aliasing control). See tests/test_pedagogy_tilt.py and
# docs/2_user_guides/problems/010_gh_example.rst.
agg GH.PoissonLevy 20 claim sev levy poisson                                                                 note{zero-parameter Levy severity; alpha=1/2 stable aggregate}

# ============================================================
# APX. Method-of-moments approximation keyword (1.0.0a47)
# ============================================================
# `approximate norm | lognorm | gamma | sgamma | slognorm` replaces the freq x
# sev convolution, at construction, with a single continuous severity fitted to
# the aggregate's moments (the shifted pair match mean, cv and skew, with
# normal as the symmetric limit and a reflected fit for left skew; lognorm and
# gamma match mean and cv only; norm matches mean and cv with zero skew),
# carried on a fixed frequency
# of 1. `exact` (or omitting the clause) is the inert default. Incompatible with
# occurrence reinsurance (the fit bypasses the per-occurrence convolution);
# aggregate reinsurance rides along. See tests/test_approximate.py,
# dev/done/plan-approximate.md and dev/done/plan-approximate-punchup.md.
agg APX.RightSgamma 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate sgamma;
agg APX.RightSlognorm 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate slognorm;
agg APX.Norm 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate norm;
agg APX.Lognorm 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate lognorm;
agg APX.Gamma 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate gamma;
agg APX.LeftReflect 1 claim sev 100 * beta 5 1.3 fixed approximate sgamma;
agg APX.AggReins 100 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 100 xs 0 approximate sgamma;
pnl APX.PnL 600000 prem less agg APX.PnL_e 5000 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate sgamma

# ============================================================
# BKT. Portfolio combine grid -- best_window (1.0.0a49)
# ============================================================
# Resolution + span combine: bs = round_bucket(max(min_k bs_k, W_tot / N)),
# replacing the legacy root-sum-square `best_bucket` (which coarsened with unit
# count and ignored the integer lattice). BucketMotiv is the headline
# signed-combine bug (RMS gave bs=2 for two bs=1 units); BucketTinyDisc is an
# all-integer book that RMS mis-sized to ~1/4096 over the full log2 cap.
# See tests/test_bucket_sizing.py and dev/done/plan-bucket-combine.md.
port BucketMotiv agg M1 dfreq[4] dsev[3] agg M2 dfreq[3] dsev[-1 1];
port BucketTinyDisc agg D1 dfreq[2] dsev[1 2] agg D2 dfreq[3] dsev[1 2 3];
port BucketFat agg F1 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg F2 80 claims sev lognorm 120 cv 1.5 poisson

# ============================================================
# WIN. Non-zero aggregate output window -- windowed sizing (1.0.0a51)
# ============================================================
# A concentrated aggregate (actual_cv < 1/z, mass band clears 0) is computed on a
# two-sided window far from 0 via benign FFT wrap: bs sizes off the band width,
# not x_max, so a high-mean book resolves finely with a non-zero x_min. Window
# is the headline 10M-claim integer case (resolves at bs=1); WinCts is the
# continuous analogue. See tests/test_bucket_sizing.py and
# dev/done/plan-bucket-window.md.
agg Window 10000000 claims dsev [1 2] poisson;
agg WinCts 100000 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 0.5 poisson

# ============================================================
# SBJ. Single-big-jump extent floor -- heavy / signed sevs (1.0.0a59)
# ============================================================
# The 3-moment MoM window is blind to a tail the first three moments miss. The
# extent (not the resolution) is floored by one big claim on a typical bulk:
# probe the severity at p** = 1 - (1-p*)/E[N], floor at ES - mu_X + q_X(p**).
# SBJ.Signed is the catastrophic case (positive agg skew hides a heavy reflected
# tail; without the floor the severity aliases and ~47% of mass is lost);
# SBJ.Heavy is a positive heavy unlimited sev (window extends when log2 allows);
# SBJ.Small is the constrained-log2 guard (keep the MoM window, honor log2).
# See tests/test_bucket_sizing.py and dev/plan-bucket-window-2.md (1A-fix).
agg SBJ.Signed 10 claims ssev 100 - lognorm 10 cv 2.5 poisson                                                note{signed heavy-left; grid must span the reach or the FFT aliases};
agg SBJ.Heavy  5000 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                      note{positive heavy unlimited; far tail ~8e5, extends when log2 allows};
agg SBJ.Small  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 2 poisson                                                          note{small heavy book, tail ~47k; constrained log2 keeps the MoM window}

# ============================================================
# USE. Tail-report-driven bucket selection (1.0.0a64)
# ============================================================
# `[use-selection]` wires the layered tail report into `_bs_window`: the SBJ
# floor is gated on a thick tail; a power-law (infinite-variance) book sizes the
# reachable bulk and truncates honestly (warn, no normalize); a concentrated
# heavy book is reclaimed by the thin-left upper-floor (former Regime B); the
# windowed slack split is tail-aware (symmetric vs thick-skewed); occurrence
# reinsurance adds a `severity (net occ)` overlay row to `tail_df`. See
# tests/test_bucket_sizing.py, tests/test_tail.py and dev/done/plan-univariate-bucket.md.
agg USE.PowerLaw   10 claims sev 100 * pareto 1.5 poisson                                                     note{infinite variance; reachable-bulk sizing + honest truncation};
agg USE.SymWindow  100000 claims sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson                                                 note{concentrated thin-tailed; symmetric slack split, convention tie-breaker};
agg USE.SignedTwoSided 20 claims dsev [-50 -10 200 5000] poisson                                              note{signed two-sided reach; padding sits between the wrapped tails};
agg USE.OccNetCapped 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of inf xs 1000 poisson                  note{unlimited 100% cession caps the net per-occurrence tail};
agg USE.OccNetFinite 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 500 xs 1000 poisson                  note{finite occ layer retains the heavy net tail};
port USE.Port agg A 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg B 50 claims sev gamma 50 cv 1 poisson         # [bs-reporting] portfolio bs_window_df / bs_description

# ============================================================
# HY3. Hygiene-3 batch: number separators + placement readings (1.0.0a52)
# ============================================================
# Item 1: Python-style `_` digit-group separators in numbers (equal to their
# underscore-free forms). Item 4 was the `of` share synonym, retired with `so`
# at 1.0.0a249; the two cases below now pin what survives it, the two readings
# of the leading quantity in a `po` clause. See tests/test_hygiene3.py and
# dev/done/plan-hygiene-3.md, dev/done/plan-single-placement-keyword.md.
agg HY3.Sep   10_000_000 claims dsev [1 2] poisson                                                          note{underscore digit separators == 10000000};
agg HY3.SepFl 1_000.5 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson                                                   note{underscore in the integer part of a float};
agg HY3.PoPct 1 claim 10000 xs 0 sev lognorm 120 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 90% po 6000 xs 4000 poisson       note{'po' percentage -> share used directly};
agg HY3.PoAmt 1 claim 10000 xs 0 sev lognorm 120 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 3000 po 6000 xs 4000 poisson      note{'po' bare amount -> amount/limit share}

# ============================================================
# SPK. Single placement keyword: what retiring 'so' freed (1.0.0a249)
# ============================================================
# `so` left the reserved-word list when the SHARE_OF terminal went, so it is an
# ordinary identifier again. The first case names an aggregate `so`; the second
# uses it as a severity name, the position where a bare word follows a keyword.
# No longer parseable, listed for reference only (commented so the corpus
# parsers skip them):
#   agg SPK.Dead 1 claim dsev [10] occurrence net of 50% so 10 xs 0 fixed
#   agg SPK.Dead 1 claim dsev [10] occurrence net of 90% of 10 xs 0 fixed
sev so dsev [1 2 3]                                                                                          note{'so' as a severity name: freed by retiring SHARE_OF};
agg so 3 claims dsev [1 2 3] poisson                                                                         note{'so' as an aggregate name; decl-testers reuses names across kinds deliberately}

# ============================================================
# DW. decl_writer unparser coverage (1.0.0a53)
# ============================================================
# The spec->DecL unparser is exercised by round-tripping the whole corpus
# (tests/test_decl_unparser.py). These two lines cover constructs not otherwise
# present: the `exposure at rate` exposure head (which canonicalizes to the
# equivalent `premium at lr` form) and a `picks` severity. See
# dev/done/plan-decl-unparser.md.
agg DW.ExpRate 1000 exposure at 0.05 rate sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                       note{exposure/rate head; renders as premium/lr};
agg DW.Picks   100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 picks [1000 5000 10000] [600 1500 900] poisson                note{picks severity layering}

# DW.PicksOffGrid is a PARSE-ONLY fixture for tests/test_picks.py: under auto
# sizing it draws bs=8, where 100 and 500 fall mid bucket, so building it
# raises the [Picks-Off-Grid-Error] ValueError by design (a319). It parses and
# round-trips like any other statement; pin bs=4 (or 0.1) to build it. DW.Picks
# above is the contrast, passing only because its auto bs=2 divides 1000, 5000
# and 10000.
agg DW.PicksOffGrid 1 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 picks [100 200 500] [45 20 25] fixed                        note{off grid picks under auto sizing: parse-only, raises on build}

# [Format-Program-Picks-Line]: the severity clause with picks is a _Block, so
# the spread layout gives picks its own line one level deeper. The terse form
# is unchanged, which is what these three pin. The trailing ! and an interior
# label both close the whole clause, so they ride at the end of the picks
# fragment and the terse byte order does not move.
agg DW.PicksLine   dfreq [1] sev 100 * uniform picks [50 75 100] [30 12 5]                                    note{picks on its own line in the spread layout, inline in terse};
agg DW.PicksBang   dfreq [1] sev 100 * uniform picks [50 75 100] [30 12 5] !                                  note{unconditional picks severity: the ! rides at the end of the picks fragment};
agg DW.PicksLabel  dfreq [1] sev 100 * uniform picks [50 75 100] [30 12 5] as Picked                          note{interior severity label after picks: closes the clause, so it rides last}


# H4. Hygiene-4 regressions — value_type, info rows, pnl prem/lr meta
# ===================================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_hygiene4.py (1.0.0a54): Portfolio.value_type
# derived from its units (mixed books rejected), fixed-layout info rows, and
# the stats_df ('meta','prem')/('meta','lr') backfill from the pnl premium
# (GROSS basis under reinsurance). See dev/done/plan-hygiene-4.md.
agg H4.Loss 5 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson                                                             note{loss unit for value_type derivation};
pnl H4.Pay1 100 prem less agg H4.Pay1_e 2 claims sev lognorm 30 cv 1 poisson                                                note{payoff unit for value_type derivation};
pnl H4.Prem 1000 prem less agg H4.Prem_e 10 claims sev lognorm 80 cv 1 poisson                                              note{pnl premium backfills meta prem/lr};
pnl H4.PremLR 1000 prem less agg H4.PremLR_e 1000 prem at 65% lr sev lognorm 80 cv 1 poisson                                               note{bare-lr pnl; exposure clause carries premium};
agg H4.ExpPrem 100 prem at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 80 cv 1 poisson                                               note{exposure-clause premium; regression guard};
pnl H4.Reins 1000 prem less agg H4.Reins_e 10 claims sev lognorm 80 cv 1 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson                note{prem/lr meta stay GROSS under reinsurance};
port H4.Book note{homogeneous loss book; portfolio value_type} agg A1 5 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson agg A2 3 claims sev gamma 20 cv .5 poisson


# UD. Unit-density accessors (numerics-1)
# =======================================
# Programs behind tests/test_unit_density.py (1.0.0a55): native-grid unit
# pmf accessors and the aligned display adapter. UD.PnLpair in the tests
# reuses the PortPnL.Pair program above (not repeated here). See
# dev/done/plan-numerics-1-unit-density.md.
port UD.Plain note{zero-origin book; aligned grid=total legacy parity} agg P 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson agg Q 30 claims sev lognorm 80 cv 1 poisson;
port UD.Disjoint note{disjoint unit windows near +/-10000} agg Hi 100 claims ssev 1 * norm + 100 poisson agg Lo 100 claims ssev 1 * norm - 100 poisson


# N2. Objective spine (numerics-2)
# ================================
# Programs behind tests/test_numerics2_objective.py (1.0.0a56): shifted-
# support kappa (exeqa_*) and the direct-sum objective columns on signed /
# zero-spectrum / legacy books, plus the Aggregate-side direct sums. See
# dev/done/plan-numerics-2-objective.md.
port N2.SignedD note{signed discrete; exact brute-force kappa incl negative origins} agg A dfreq [1 2] dsev [-3 -1 2] [.5 .3 .2] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [-2 1 4] agg C dfreq [2] dsev [1 3];
port N2.ZeroSpec note{symmetric line zeroes FFT bins; prefix/suffix ft_nots branch} agg S dfreq [1] dsev [-1 1] agg T dfreq [1] dsev [1 2] agg U dfreq [1 2] dsev [2 3];
port N2.Legacy note{zero-origin book; sum exa_i == exa_total; F/S guards} agg A dfreq [0 1 2] dsev [1 2 3] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [2 4] [.6 .4];
port N2.PosOrigin note{base for the hand-built positive-origin add_exa frame} agg A dfreq [1] dsev [10 11] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [20 22] [.6 .4];
port N2.SignedLN note{signed continuous; exeqa vs Monte Carlo incl a<0} agg A 8 claims ssev 20 * lognorm 0.4 - 25 poisson agg L 4 claims sev lognorm 30 cv 0.4 poisson;
agg N2.Dice dfreq [1] dsev [1:6]                                                                                  note{hand checks: lev(3)=2.5 exlea(3)=2 exgta(3)=5};
pnl N2.PnL 10 prem less agg N2.PnL_e dfreq [1] dsev [2 5] [.5 .5]                                                               note{signed Aggregate direct sums carry the origin}


# N3. Distortion spine (numerics-3)
# =================================
# Programs behind tests/test_numerics3_distortion.py (1.0.0a57): the exact-
# discrete Choquet helper, the view x value_type 2x2, the unified linear/
# lifted builder, builder-level mass guard, signed total pricing, and the
# on-demand per-line capital. See dev/done/plan-numerics-3-distortion.md.
agg N3.Dice dfreq [1:6] dsev [1:6]                                                                                note{six-surface consistency target; choquet hand checks};
port N3.OneDice note{one-line port: line == total} agg D dfreq [1:6] dsev [1:6];
port N3.Bodoff note{bounded discrete; linear vs lifted; mass distortion exact} agg wind  1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0,  99] [0.80, 0.20] fixed agg quake 1 claim sev dhistogram xps [0, 100] [0.95, 0.05] fixed;
port N3.SignedD note{signed totals price via dot(kappa, gp); shares NaN} agg A dfreq [1 2] dsev [-3 -1 2] [.5 .3 .2] agg B dfreq [1] dsev [-2 1 4] agg C dfreq [2] dsev [1 3];
port N3.PayPair note{homogeneous payoff book (orientation suffix) prices through the dual} agg P1 dfreq [1] dsev [5 8] [.5 .5] payoff agg P2 dfreq [1] dsev [5 7] [.5 .5] payoff;
port N3.Unb note{mass-on-unbounded refused in the lifted builder (G6)} agg U 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson

# ============================================================
# 1P. Portfolio windowed combine (1.0.0a66)
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_bucket_sizing.py 1P block: the portfolio combine
# reconciled to Portfolio MM (bulk span from the total moments, not a width
# sum) + single-big-jump look-through (max_k, not sum) + windowed non-signed
# origin (Plan B). See dev/done/plan-bucket-window-2.md Round 3.
port BW.DivIID note{diversified iid: MM sits ~sqrt(k) tighter than the legacy linear sum} agg U1 200 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1.2 poisson agg U2 200 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1.2 poisson agg U3 200 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1.2 poisson agg U4 200 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1.2 poisson;
port BW.Heavy note{heavy unit drives the SBJ look-through extent floor} agg HU 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg LU 50 claims sev gamma 50 cv 1 poisson;
port BW.Windowed note{concentrated total clears 0: windowed Plan B origin x_min>0} agg H1 5000 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 0.5 poisson agg H2 4000 claims sev gamma 10 cv 0.5 poisson;
port BW.Mixed note{signed combine; resolution floor keeps bs=1, no wrap} agg M1 dfreq [4] dsev [3] agg M2 dfreq [3] dsev [-1 1]

# ============================================================
# SC. Signed / payoff distortion calibration (1.0.0a99)
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_signed_calibrate.py: calibrate_distortions runs
# every subclass calibrate on the canonical non-negative loss frame Z (reverse
# a payoff, then shift by c = max(0, -min support)); the receipt un-shifts back
# to the caller's loss convention. Shift-exactness (Base0 vs Signed), the payoff
# dual round-trip (Pnl), and the per-kind sweep on a signed grid. See
# dev/plan-signed-distortion-calibration.md.
agg SC.Base0  dfreq [1] dsev [0 10 20] [.5 .3 .2]                                                                 note{non-negative base anchored at 0: classic c=0 path};
agg SC.Signed dfreq [1] dsev [-10 0 10] [.5 .3 .2]                                                                note{Base0 slid -10: signed loss, shift-exact twin};
pnl SC.Pnl    10 prem less agg SC.Pnl_e dfreq [1] dsev [2 5 12] [.4 .4 .2]                                                      note{payoff: reversed then shifted; ask price = -P via g_dual};
agg SC.Classic 50 claims sev lognorm 40 cv 1.5 poisson                                                            note{classic non-negative loss: transform branch never entered}

# ============================================================
# EV. Margin acceptability: evaluate solves rho_g(margin) = 0
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_distortion_calibrate.py and tests/test_pnl.py.
# `evaluate` calibrates each family to the canonical shift constant, so the
# breakeven `gini_p` is the Cherny and Madan acceptability index. Constant
# premium is the case where it collapses back to the classic rho_g(L) = P;
# a loss-sensitive premium is where the two part company. See
# dev/done/plan-margin-acceptability-evaluate.md.
agg EV.Agg   1000 premium at 0.7 lr sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson                                                  note{Aggregate.evaluate default P = exp_premium};
agg EV.NoPrem 100 claims sev gamma 10 cv 1 poisson                                                                note{no premium declared: evaluate raises rather than guessing};
pnl EV.Pnl   1000 prem less agg EV.Pnl_e 1000 prem at 0.7 lr sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson                         note{constant premium: agrees with the Aggregate face};
pnl EV.Arb   1000 prem less agg EV.Arb_e dfreq [1] dsev [1 2]                                                     note{M >= 0 a.s.: acceptable at every stress, reports NaN};
port EV.Port agg EV.U1 1000 premium at 0.65 lr sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson agg EV.U2 500 premium at 0.75 lr sev gamma 50 cv 1.2 poisson   note{unit profile: the book beats both units};
xpnl EV.Peel 1000 premium less agg EV.Peel_e 1000 premium at 0.7 lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 200 xs 600 deposit 50 and 400 xs 800 deposit 30 peel top-down  note{tower: gini_p rises down the running nets as cover is bought};
xpnl EV.CedeTower 33333.33333333321 premium as GWP less agg EV.CedeTower_e as Subject 2 claims sev 20000 * uniform occurrence net of 50% po 5000 xs 10000 rate 0.3 cede 0.3 as "Occ1" and 50% po 5000 xs 15000 rate 0.2 cede 0.3 as "Occ2" fixed aggregate net of 2500 xs 23500 rol 0.25 as "Agg1" and 2000 xs 26000 rol 0.15 as "Agg2" less 5% loss expense as LAE 500 fixed expense as "Fixed Exp" 20% premium expense as "Acq Exp" peel top-down  note{two tiers priced to leave the reinsurer a margin: every ceded layer evaluates from the seller's side}

# ============================================================
# UM. DecL unary minus on a severity (1.0.0a109)
# ============================================================
# Bare unary minus `ssev -X` reflects the severity, sugar for the working
# `ssev 0 - X`. Unary minus binds tighter than the additive shift, so
# `-lognorm 2 + 5` is `(-X) + 5 == 5 - X`. Round-trips via the canonical
# `-1 * X` scaled form. See tests/test_decl_parser.py and
# dev/done/plan-decl-sev-unary-minus.md. Reflection under the clamped `sev`
# clause is the RS section below (it was rejected outright until 1.0.0a230).
agg UM.Negate 10 claims ssev -lognorm 10 cv 0.5 poisson                                                          note{bare unary minus reflects; parity with `ssev 0 - lognorm`};
agg UM.Shift  10 claims ssev -lognorm 2 cv 0.5 + 5 poisson                                                       note{tight bind: (-X) + 5 == 5 - X, mean 3};
agg UM.Scaled 10 claims ssev -3 * lognorm 2 cv 0.5 poisson                                                       note{negative NUMBER -3 stays on the scaled path, mean -6}

# ============================================================
# EXP. P&L gross expenses + ceded premium / commission (Phase 1)
# ============================================================
# Gross expenses on a pnl in three explicit forms (decision 1): a fraction of
# premium, a fraction of expected loss, or a fixed amount; expense/expenses both
# accepted. Multiple expense terms join with `and` and sum (EXP.MultiExp). Ceded
# premium per reinsurance layer in one of deposit | rol | rate
# (decision 3): deposit a currency amount, rol = share x rol x limit, rate =
# rate x gross premium; an optional cede books the ceding commission as an
# expense credit. Any premium clause promotes the pnl to the Gross/Ceded/Net
# exhibit. See tests/test_pnl_expenses.py, tests/test_pnl_ceded_premium.py and
# dev/done/plan-pnl-expenses-ceded-premium.md.
pnl EXP.PremExp  1000 prem less agg EXP.PremExp_e 8 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses                     note{variable expense: 25% of premium};
pnl EXP.LossExp  1000 prem less agg EXP.LossExp_e 8 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson less 0.3 loss expenses                         note{variable expense: 30% of expected loss};
pnl EXP.FixedExp 1000 prem less agg EXP.FixedExp_e 8 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson less 200 fixed expenses                        note{fixed expense amount};
pnl EXP.Deposit  5000 prem less agg EXP.Deposit_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 deposit 1500         note{ceded premium as a deposit amount};
pnl EXP.RolCede  5000 prem less agg EXP.RolCede_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 rol 0.08 cede 0.25   note{rate-on-line premium + ceding commission};
pnl EXP.Rate     5000 prem less agg EXP.Rate_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 rate 0.3            note{ceded premium as a rate on gross premium};
pnl EXP.OccRol   5000 prem less agg EXP.OccRol_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 100 xs 200 rol 0.1 poisson             note{occurrence-layer ceded premium; occ-only GCN};
pnl EXP.Both     5000 prem less agg EXP.Both_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 100 xs 200 rol 0.05 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 rol 0.08 cede 0.2 less 200 fixed expenses note{both sides priced + fixed expense};
pnl EXP.MultiExp 1000 prem less agg EXP.MultiExp_e 8 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson less 25% premium expense and 200 fixed expense              note{multiple expense terms joined by `and`, summed}

# ============================================================
# Property-cat reinstatement premiums (stochastic ceded premium). A
# `reinstatements` clause decorates ONE occurrence layer carrying a base premium
# (deposit | rol | rate); the price multipliers alpha_j (relative to the base
# rate on line r = base_premium / limit) are given either as the treaty-language
# group chain `<count> free and <count> at <p>%` (counts as digits or the
# number-words one..five) or the explicit list `[a1 a2 ...]`. Both canonicalize
# to the list form on unparse. The annual recovery cap (m+1)y lives in the
# ReinstatementTerms, never in the occurrence layer. build() returns a PnL whose
# GCN exhibit is backed by a ReinstatementAnalysis. See
# tests/test_reinstatement_decl.py and dev/plan-reinstatements.md.
pnl RI.Human 10000 premium less agg RI.Human_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence net of 95% po 100 xs 100 rol 18% reinstatements 1 free and 1 at 50% and 2 at 100% poisson   note{treaty-language schedule: alpha = [0 .5 1 1]};
pnl RI.Words 10000 premium less agg RI.Words_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rol 18% reinstatements one free and three at 100% poisson                  note{number-word counts: alpha = [0 1 1 1]};
pnl RI.List  10000 premium less agg RI.List_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rol 18% reinstatements [0 .5 1 1] poisson                                   note{explicit price-multiplier list escape hatch};
pnl RI.Ceded 10000 premium less agg RI.Ceded_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence ceded to 100 xs 100 deposit 1800 reinstatements [0 1 1] poisson                               note{occurrence ceded to + deposit base premium};
pnl RI.AggCover 10000 premium less agg RI.AggCover_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rol 18% reinstatements [0 1] poisson aggregate net of 85% po 1500 xs 7000note{decision 3: reinstated occ layer + subsequent aggregate cover stays 2-D};
pnl RI.NoReinst 10000 premium less agg RI.NoReinst_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rol 18% no reinstatements poisson                                        note{zero reinstatements: single annual limit y, deterministic ceded premium};

# ============================================================
# Variable rating (Phase 3, decision 0: at most ONE variable feature per program,
# no stacking). swing / slide / pc / corridor decorate ONE aggregate layer; each
# makes one P&L leg stochastic via a ContractTerms pushed forward over the gross
# density (1-D aggregate basis). build() returns a PnL whose GCN exhibit is the
# group ledger. See tests/test_variable_rating_decl.py and
# dev/plan-variable-rating.md.
#   * swing    replaces deposit/rol/rate: ceded premium = clip(basic + lcm*A, min, max)
#   * slide    replaces cede: commission slides down a ceded-LR scale from anchors
#   * pc       profit commission share*(1 - LR - allowance)+ of ceded premium
#   * corridor a ceded-LR band (po width xs attach) the cedant retains
pnl VR.Swing    10000 premium less agg VR.Swing_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson aggregate net of 5000 xs 4000 swing basic 500 lcm 0.5 min 500 max 3000                          note{swing: stochastic ceded premium of ceded loss};
pnl VR.Slide    10000 premium less agg VR.Slide_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson aggregate net of 5000 xs 4000 deposit 1500 slide 45% at 60% and 25% at 70% and 19% at 80%      note{slide: stochastic ceding commission of ceded LR};
pnl VR.PC       10000 premium less agg VR.PC_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson aggregate net of 5000 xs 4000 deposit 1500 pc 25% after 10%                                    note{profit commission of ceded LR};
pnl VR.Corridor 10000 premium less agg VR.Corridor_e 10000 prem at 85% lr sev lognorm 50 cv 3 poisson aggregate net of 5000 xs 4000 deposit 1500 corridor 50% po 30% xs 20%                          note{corridor: cedant retains a ceded-LR band};
# retro is the account-level rating clause: it replaces the fixed `<n> premium`
# head with `retro <collar> premium`, varying the GROSS premium as a collared
# affine map of net account loss (no inuring reinsurance in the 1-D case). Collar
# is keyword-first, matching swing.
pnl VR.Retro retro basic 3000 lcm 1.1 min 3500 max 8000 premium less agg VR.Retro_e 1000 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                          note{retro: stochastic gross premium of net account loss}

# ============================================================
# Y. DecL labels & expense grouping                  (tests/test_decl_labels.py)
# ============================================================
# The `as` display-label clause: a bareword skips the quote tax, a "quoted"
# label carries spaces. `name` stays the identity handle; the label is
# presentation only (the resolved `label` / exhibit titles). Also the
# two-level expense grammar: `and` combines terms into one leg, juxtaposition
# makes separate legs, each with an optional `as` label. See dev/plan-decl-labels.md.
agg LB.GrossBook as "Gross Book P&L" 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                          note{quoted object display label};
agg LB.Bareword as lae 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                        note{bareword label skips the quote tax};
sev LB.Sev as "My Severity" lognorm 100 cv 2                                                                          note{sev object display label};
pnl LB.Book as "My Book" 1000 premium as GWP less agg LB.Book_e 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson                             note{object label + premium (consideration) label};
pnl LB.OneGroup 1000 premium less agg LB.OneGroup_e 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson less 25% premium expense and 200 fixed expense   note{and-joined -> one combined expense leg (back-compat)};
pnl LB.TwoGroups 1000 premium less agg LB.TwoGroups_e 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson less 25% premium expense 200 fixed expense      note{juxtaposition -> two separate expense legs};
pnl LB.Labeled 1000 premium less agg LB.Labeled_e 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson less 25% premium expense as acq 30% loss expense as lae note{labeled expense groups};
pnl LB.CatXL 1000 premium less agg LB.CatXL_e 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 occurrence net of 100 xs 200 deposit 50 as "Cat XL" poisson  note{reins-cession label -> cession leg / group rows in the ledger};
# [DecL-Labels-Everywhere]: interior sub-object labels -> Aggregate.label_map,
# read back through the `labels` namespace (a.labels.exposure / .layer / .severity).
# exposure label sits mid-clause (after the amount, before `at ... lr/rate`);
# layer and inline-severity labels append after their clause. See dev/plan-labels.md.
agg LB.Exposure 10000 premium as "GWP 2026" at 0.65 lr 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                          note{exposure label, mid-clause -> label_map[exposure]};
agg LB.Claims 100 claims as "Claim count" sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                 note{claims exposure label, end-clause};
agg LB.Layer 100 claims 1000 xs 500 as "Working Layer" sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                    note{layer label -> label_map[layer]; S2 ambiguity guard vs following sev};
agg LB.SevClause 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 as "ISO ME B" poisson                                                 note{inline severity-clause label -> label_map[severity]};
agg LB.AllInterior 10000 premium as GWP at 0.65 lr 1000 xs 500 as "Layer 1" sev lognorm 100 cv 2 as "ISO" poisson      note{all three interior labels combine};

# ============================================================
# Z. Reins economics on a plain agg (ignored + warned)
#                                   (tests/test_agg_ignored_clauses.py)
# ============================================================
# [Reins-Economics-On-Agg-Ignore-Warn]: a pure aggregate accepts every
# reinsurance decoration -- ceded premium (deposit / rol / rate), cede,
# reinstatements, variable-rating features -- builds the loss structure only,
# and emits one IgnoredDecLClauseWarning naming the ignored clauses. The
# knowledge base keeps the full spec, so `pnl X <prem> less agg.NAME`
# re-injects the economics (the knowledge-injection route). See
# dev/plan-pnl-consolidated-xpnl-walk.md Phase 1.
agg IG.Deposit 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 deposit 160 cede 25%          note{ceded premium + cede on a plain agg: ignored with warning, retained for injection};
agg IG.Rate 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 rate 30%                          note{rate has no base on a bare agg; resolves against the premium of a wrapping pnl};
agg IG.Reinst 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 500 xs 500 deposit 150 reinstatements 1 at 100% poisson  note{reinstatement schedule on a plain agg: ignored with warning};
agg IG.Swing 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000 swing basic 500 lcm 0.5          note{variable-rating feature on a plain agg: ignored with warning};

# [Reins-Economics-On-Bvagg-Ignore-Warn]: the same clauses reach a bivariate on
# its component specs, and both unit-construction routes splat the unit spec
# into Aggregate. The factory filters per unit, naming the unit, with the
# joint's own remedy: a joint carries loss against loss, so there is nothing to
# fold it into. The cession layers stay, since the netceded joint needs them.
grossceded agg IG.BvDeposit dfreq [3] dsev [2 4 6 8 10 12] occurrence net of 6 xs 6 deposit 5                        note{ceded premium under a view-pair prefix: the GCN control shape, ignored with warning};
bivariate IG.BvCopula 5 claims agg IG.BvA dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] dsev [2 4 6 8 10 12] occurrence net of 6 xs 6 deposit 5 agg IG.BvB dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] dsev [1 2 3 4] copula normal 0.5   note{ceded premium on a copula component: ignored with warning naming the unit};

# ============================================================
# AA. The pnl/xpnl composition matrix ([One-Classifier-Fix])
#                                   (tests/test_composition_matrix.py)
# ============================================================
# Two-tier classification (dev/done/plan-pnl-faces-punchlist.md): the occurrence tier
# {none | gc | reinstatements} and the aggregate tier {none | gc | feature}
# classify independently and dispatch on the pair. The previously
# silently-wrong cells: a feature on the aggregate cover composed with an
# inuring occurrence program -- guaranteed-cost
# ([Var-Feature-Composed-With-Occ-Program]) or reinstated
# ([Reinstatements-Dropped-By-Feature-Branch]) -- plus zero-premium cessions
# (booked at zero ceded premium with a ZeroPremiumCessionWarning) and the
# one-step plain walk ([Decision-XPnL-Plain-Is-One-Step-Walk]).
pnl CM.GcFeat inherit premium less agg CM.GcFeat_e 12500 premium at 80% lr 5000 xs 0 sev 200 * pareto 1.6 - 200 occurrence net of 4750 xs 250 rate 55% cede 10% as "Cat Program" poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 3100 swing basic 100 lcm 1.5 min 200 max 500 as "Swing Program"    note{GC occ + swing agg: consolidated folds - pc_occ + c_occ into the net premium; the feature's subject is the net-of-occ aggregate};
xpnl CM.GcFeatWalk inherit premium less agg CM.GcFeatWalk_e 12500 premium at 80% lr 5000 xs 0 sev 200 * pareto 1.6 - 200 occurrence net of 4750 xs 250 rate 55% cede 10% as "Cat Program" poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 3100 swing basic 100 lcm 1.5 min 200 max 500 as "Swing Program"    note{the stitched walk: gross -> ceded occ -> feature cover -> Total; feature rows are pushforwards of the net-occ marginal};
xpnl CM.ReinstFeat inherit premium less agg CM.ReinstFeat_e 12500 premium at 80% lr 5000 xs 0 sev 200 * pareto 1.6 - 200 occurrence net of 4750 xs 250 rate 55% no reinstatements as "Cat Program" poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 3100 swing basic 100 lcm 1.5 min 200 max 500 as "Swing Program"    note{reinstated occ + swing agg: the reinstatement branch wins; the swing map rides the same (L, R) joint; the annual cap survives};
pnl CM.ZeroPrem 5000 premium less agg CM.ZeroPrem_e 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 poisson aggregate net of 2000 xs 3000    note{cession with no premium clause: consolidated at zero ceded premium + ZeroPremiumCessionWarning};
xpnl CM.OneStep 1000 premium less agg CM.OneStep_e 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson    note{xpnl over a plain engine: the trivial one-step walk (gross -> Total, impact 0)};

# ============================================================
# AB. Renewal wait-clause frequencies ([Renewal-Frequency-Wait-Clause])
#     (tests/test_renewal_decl.py, tests/test_renewal_agg.py)
# ============================================================
# Sparre-Andersen renewal claim generation: a "T years" exposure head paired
# (strictly, at the grammar level) with a wait/dwait waiting-time law in the
# freq slot. exp_en carries the -1 empirical sentinel; the count pmf is the
# renewal count N(T). "at NUMBER rate" books informational premium T*rate.
agg AB.Renewal.Expon 10 years sev lognorm 100 cv 1 wait expon                                 note{Equivalent to "10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson"};
agg AB.Renewal.Rate 1 year at 500 rate sev lognorm 100 cv 1 wait 0.1 * expon                  note{Rate 10/yr for 1 year; exp_premium = 500 informational, feeds PnL gross premium};
agg AB.Renewal.Gamma 5 years sev lognorm 10 cv .3 wait gamma 2                                note{Gamma(2) waits: P(N>=k) = gammainc(2k, T)};
agg AB.Renewal.Uniform 1 year dsev [1] wait uniform                                           note{Uniform(0,1) waits at T=1: P(N=k) = k/(k+1)!};
agg AB.Renewal.Determin 3 years dsev [1] dwait [1]                                            note{Deterministic unit waits: N == 3 (atom exactly at T counts)};
agg AB.Renewal.Cluster 2 years dsev [1] dwait [0 1] [.5 .5]                                   note{Zero waits = geometric claim clusters; P(N=n) = C(n,2)/8 * (1/2)^(n-2)};
agg AB.Renewal.Defective 2 years dsev [1] dwait [1 2] [.4 .5] !                               note{Defective (terminating) renewal process, defect 0.1};
agg AB.Renewal.Splice 2 years sev lognorm 10 cv .3 wait expon splice [0 1.1] !                note{Unconditional splice window: waits beyond 1.1 terminate the process};
agg AB.Renewal.Mixture 5 years sev gamma 10 cv .3 wait [.6 .4] * expon wts [.5 .5]            note{Hyperexponential mixture wait};
agg AB.Renewal.Labels 10 years as decade dsev [1] dwait [1:3] as slow_waits                   note{Interior labels: exposure + wait sites pool into label_map};
agg AB.Renewal.Layer 10 years sev lognorm 100 cv 1 wait 2 xs 0.25 expon                       note{Conditional layered wait (W-a given W>a, capped at 2); cap atom at 2 snaps the grid};
agg AB.Renewal.LayerCl 10 years sev lognorm 100 cv 1 wait 2 xs 0.25 expon !                   note{Unconditional layered wait min((W-a)+, 2); P(W<=a) clusters at 0};
agg AB.Renewal.LayerMix 5 years sev gamma 10 cv .3 wait [1 2] xs [0 0.5] expon wts [.6 .4]    note{Broadcast vector layer: mixture of differently layered waits};

# ============================================================
# AC. Zero-truncated / zero-modified frequency ([ZT-ZM-Frequency-Fix])
#     (tests/test_frequency_zm.py)
# ============================================================
# The (a, b, 1) class of Klugman-Panjer-Willmot 2012 section 6.6. The exposure
# clause sets the UN-MODIFIED (base) mean and the reweighting shifts it
# forward, so the realized E[N] is an output -- the textbook parameterization,
# matching Loss Data Analytics and R's actuar. A trailing "!" pins the mean
# instead: solve for the base mean whose realized E[N] equals the clause.
agg AC.ZM.Poisson 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zm 0.5 !                                            note{The ! reading: 4 is the base mean, Poisson(4) reweighted to p0=0.5, so the realized E[N] = 2.0373, not 4};
agg AC.ZM.PoissonPin 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zm 0.5                                       note{The default reading: E[N] = 4 exactly, the base mean solving to 7.9973};
agg AC.ZT.Poisson 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zt !                                                note{Zero-truncated Poisson(4): E[N] = 4/(1-exp(-4)) = 4.0746};
agg AC.ZT.PoissonSmall 0.5 claims dsev [1] poisson zt !                                         note{Base mean below 1 is fine; the ZT mean is 1.2707 (a ZT count cannot average below 1)};
agg AC.ZT.PoissonPin 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zt                                           note{Pinned ZT: E[N] = 4, base mean 3.9207};
agg AC.ZM.Deflate 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zm 0.005 !                                          note{Zero DEFLATION: p0 below the natural exp(-4) = 0.0183 raises the mean};
agg AC.ZM.Negbin 4 claims dsev [1] negbin 3 zm .5 !                                             note{ZM negative binomial; freq_a is the variance multiplier 1+beta};
agg AC.ZM.Geometric 4 claims dsev [1] geometric zm .5 !                                         note{ZM geometric};
agg AC.ZM.Binomial 4 claims dsev [1] binomial 0.6 zm .5 !                                       note{ZM binomial, per-trial probability 0.6};
agg AC.ZM.Logarithmic 4 claims dsev [1] logarithmic zm .5 !                                     note{Logarithmic already has p0 = 0, so zm only adds mass at zero};
agg AC.ZM.LossPin 1000 loss sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson zm 0.5                              note{Monetary exposure, default reading: hits E[X] = 1000; append ! and it warns and delivers 500};

# [ZT-ZM-Recalibrate-Default] (a325): create_frequency renders its child in the
# explicit base-parameterization form whichever way the parent was written, so
# the modification is applied once. The pinned parent is the case that broke,
# reporting 100 / (1 - 0.3) = 142.857 against the parent's own 100.
agg AC.ZM.Count    100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson zm 0.3                             note{pinned zm parent: create_frequency must reproduce E[N] = 100, not 142.857};
agg AC.ZM.CountB   100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson zm 0.3 !                           note{the same parent under the ! reading: 100 is the base mean};
agg AC.ZT.Count    100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson zt                                 note{pinned zt parent, the truncated twin};
agg AC.ZT.CountB   100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1 poisson zt !                               note{zt under the ! reading: 100 is the base mean}

# ============================================================
# AD. Eventual-ruin solvers ([Ruin-Wiener-Hopf])
#     (tests/test_ruin.py)
# ============================================================
# pollaczeck_khinchine requires poisson (guard); wiener_hopf requires a
# renewal wait-clause frequency and a proper (non-defective) wait law.
# AD.Ruin.Poisson and AD.Ruin.Renewal are the SAME model, the WH-vs-PK
# cross-validation anchor; AD.Ruin.ExponSev is the non-Poisson Lundberg
# closed form psi(u) = psi(0) exp(-beta (1 - psi(0)) u).
agg AD.Ruin.Poisson 10 claims sev gamma 2 poisson                                             note{PK path; equivalent to AD.Ruin.Renewal (exponential waits = compound Poisson)};
agg AD.Ruin.Renewal 10 years sev gamma 2 wait expon                                           note{WH path; same model as AD.Ruin.Poisson};
agg AD.Ruin.ExponSev 5 years sev 10 * expon wait gamma 2                                      note{Exponential severity, gamma-2 waits: exact geometric ladder heights, Lundberg slope beta (1 - psi(0))};
agg AD.Ruin.MixWait 5 years sev 10 * expon wait [.6 .4] * expon wts [.5 .5]                   note{Hyperexponential mixture wait through the multi-component _discretize_wait_pmf path; Lundberg form still exact};
agg AD.Ruin.Fixed 10 claims sev gamma 2 fixed                                                 note{Rejected by BOTH solvers (neither poisson nor renewal)};
agg AD.Ruin.Binomial 10 claims sev gamma 2 binomial 0.5                                       note{Rejected by pollaczeck_khinchine (strict poisson guard)};
agg AD.Ruin.Defective 2 years dsev [1] dwait [1 2] [.4 .5] !                                  note{Rejected by wiener_hopf: defective (terminating) wait law};

# ============================================================
# AE. Trailer attachment ([Recipe-Library])
#     (tests/test_trailer_attachment.py, tests/test_grammar_ambiguity.py)
# ============================================================
# Where a TRAILING trailer binds when the statement nests an object that ends
# in its own trailer. The rule, in one line: a trailing trailer always binds to
# the OUTER object; to annotate an inner component, put its trailer on that
# component. These fixtures pin it.
#
# AE.Trailer.BivariateBare is the one genuinely ambiguous shape in the grammar:
# copula_clause is nullable and bv_body ends in an agg_out that carries its own
# trailer, so with neither a copula clause nor an outer frequency to separate
# them, `note{...}` can parse as either. Earley's ambiguity='resolve' picks the
# outer binding, which is the documented behaviour; test_grammar_ambiguity.py
# holds the line. Adding a copula or a frequency clause removes the ambiguity
# entirely (AE.Trailer.BivariateSeparated).
#
# port is NOT ambiguous: port_out places its trailer BEFORE agg_list, so the
# port's slot has already closed by the time a unit is read -- which is why a
# note written after the last unit lands on that unit, not the portfolio.
bivariate AE.Trailer.BivariateBare 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 note{ambiguous shape: binds to the bivariate};
bivariate AE.Trailer.BivariateSeparated 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson note{copula and freq separate the trailer: unambiguous};
bivariate AE.Trailer.BivariateInner 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 note{inner wind} agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 note{inner flood} copula gumbel 0.4 poisson note{outer bivariate};
netceded agg AE.Trailer.NetCeded 8 claims sev 300 * beta 2 3 occurrence net of 0.7 po 60 xs 40 poisson note{binds to the netceded view-pair};
clash AE.Trailer.Clash 8 5 2 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.2 sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 mixed gamma 0.2 note{binds to the clash};
port AE.Trailer.Port note{on the portfolio} agg AE.Trailer.PortUnit 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson note{on the last unit}

# AE.Trailer.RefSource is the entry a builtin reference points at, for
# tests/test_reference_trailer.py: a reference stands for the stored entry, so
# an absent outer clause must leave the stored note, tags and hints in place
# (a318, [Reference-Trailer-Preservation]). bs=125 is non dyadic on purpose, so
# a reference that loses the hints lands on a visibly different grid instead of
# agreeing with the auto sized one by luck. The reference statements themselves
# are NOT mirrored here: the unparser resolves `agg.NAME` back to the full spec,
# so they are not round-trip fixed points ([Unparser-Reference-Gaps]).
agg AE.Trailer.RefSource 1 claim sev lognorm 100 cv 2 fixed note{the stored trailer a reference must preserve} tags{topic:reference} hints{bs=125; log2=18}

# ============================================================
# AF. tags{...} ([Recipe-Library])
#     (tests/test_trailer_clauses.py)
# ============================================================
# tags{} is a comma- and/or space-separated slug list, decomposed to a tuple.
# A fourth trailer clause, doc{{{...}}}, carried a long-form markdown recipe
# beside it until 1.0.0a301, when it was retired
# (dev/done/plan-decommission-docs.md): a trailer states facts about an entry,
# and a book is not a fact about an entry. Since 1.0.0a177 a note{} body is
# lifted behind a placeholder (step 0b), so free text in a note is no longer
# the constraint it once was: see section AG.
agg AF.Tags.Spaces 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                       tags{severity frequency aggregate}                note{space-separated tag list};
agg AF.Tags.Commas 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                       tags{severity, frequency, aggregate}              note{comma-separated tag list};
agg AF.Tags.Mixed  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                       tags{severity,frequency  aggregate, role:reference}  note{mixed separators and a namespaced tag};
dist AF.Tags.Dist ph 0.7                                                                      tags{distortion, hero}                            note{distortions carry a trailer since 1.0.0a157};


# ============================================================
# AG. note/tags/hints bodies are free text (1.0.0a177)
#     (tests/test_doc_clause.py)
# ============================================================
# A note is prose, so it may hold the three characters DecL preprocessing used
# to claim: `#` and `//` (the comment openers, step 2) and `[` / `]` (the
# vector collapse, step 3). Step 0b lifts each single-line note/tags/hints body
# behind an indexed placeholder and step 7 puts it back verbatim, so what
# reaches the lexer is what was typed. Before that, `#` and `//` truncated the
# statement into a parse error several clauses upstream, and brackets were
# silently padded: `E[loss]=85` was stored as `E [loss] =85`, and the padding
# grew by one space every time the file was regenerated from its own specs.
# Two things stay forbidden in a note, and there is no longer a longer form to
# escape to: a `}` (the terminal ends at the first one) and a line break. A
# note is one line of prose about the entry.
agg AG.Note.Brackets  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{E[loss]=85, margin 15};
agg AG.Note.Hash      5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{layer is 5# of limit};
agg AG.Note.Slashes   5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{net // ceded, a ratio not a comment};
agg AG.Note.Semicolon 5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{alpha = [0 .5 1 1]; beta follows};
agg AG.Note.Trailing  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{ends on a hash #};
agg AG.Note.WithRest  5 claims sev lognorm 10 cv 1 poisson                                     note{5# of limit}  tags{topic:numerics}  hints{log2=16};

# ============================================================
# AH. OEP curve fixtures ([OEP-Curve], 1.0.0a180)
#     (tests/test_oep.py)
# ============================================================
# `oep(agg, p)` inverts OEP(x) = 1 - exp(-lam Pr(L > x)): given an annual
# probability it returns the loss one or more occurrences exceed with that
# probability. It reads the EXACT severity through `agg.sev.isf`, never the
# bucketed `q_sev`, so these fixtures are about severity shape and frequency
# kind, not about the FFT grid; most are built with update=False.
#
# AH.Blog is `ss.lognorm(1, scale=1000)` written in DecL's (mean, cv) form,
# full precision on purpose: the published losses are quoted to three decimals
# and a truncated cv misses them. AH.Mix forces the mixture branch, where the
# inverse has no closed form and is solved by bracketed brentq. AH.Limit has an
# atom at the limit, where the achieved oep falls below the requested p. The
# rest are the frequencies `oep` must refuse.
agg AH.Blog    2 claims sev lognorm 1648.7212707001281 cv 1.3108324944320862 poisson           note{Return Period Confusions Clarified, Table 8: p=.001 -> 26853.227};
agg AH.Mix     [1 2] claims sev lognorm [1000 5000] cv [1 2] wts [.6 .4] poisson               note{Four-component mixture; sev.isf(.01) = 26880.5485 against q_sev 26880};
agg AH.Snap    2 claims sev lognorm 1000 cv 1.31 poisson                                       note{sev.ppf(.99) = 6207.853 against the snapped q_sev(.99) = 6208 at bs=8};
agg AH.Limit   2 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 1000 cv 2 poisson                                note{Atom at the limit: S_sev = 0, oep = 0, both return periods infinite};
agg AH.Negbin  2 claims sev lognorm 1000 cv 2 negbin 2                                         note{Refused: Poisson thinning is what makes 1 - exp(-lam S) hold};
agg AH.Fixed   2 claims sev lognorm 1000 cv 2 fixed                                            note{Refused: fixed count};
agg AH.Mixed   2 claims sev lognorm 1000 cv 2 mixed gamma 0.3                                  note{Refused: gamma-mixed Poisson is not Poisson};
agg AH.ZM      2 claims sev lognorm 1000 cv 2 poisson zm 0.5 !                                   note{Refused: zero modification breaks P(N=0) = exp(-lam)};

# ============================================================
# AI. Layer peeling ([Layer-Peeling-Shorthand], 1.0.0a183)
#     (tests/test_pnl_peel.py)
# ============================================================
# `xpnl ... peel top-down|bottom-up` books one group per reinsurance LAYER
# instead of one per tier, so every layer reports its own ceded premium,
# ceding commission and marginal impact. Occurrence layers are peeled before
# aggregate ones, preserving the tier walk's step order; zero-share gap fillers
# are structural and get no step. See dev/done/plan-layer-peeling.md.
#
# Two routes. AI.Agg peels aggregate layers only: those are disjoint intervals
# on the one aggregate subject, so single-layer ceders sum to the cumulative
# ceder identically, every leg stays per-atom and the scenario (kappa) ladder
# holds. AI.Occ peels two occurrence layers, which no shared source can carry
# (the random claim count decouples the ceded-occurrence aggregate from the
# gross one), so its rows are supplied as exact marginals: EX foots exactly by
# linearity, the dispersion columns are marginal. AI.One has one layer per tier
# and so reproduces the plain tier walk in either direction.
#
# Tier subtotals ([Tier-Subtotal-Rows], 1.0.0a184): a tier that peels into two
# or more steps also gets its own "All occurrence" / "All aggregate" block after
# its last layer, so a peeled tower still shows the whole program. AI.Both has
# two layers in each tier and so carries both blocks; AI.One carries neither,
# since a one-step tier already is its own subtotal.
xpnl AI.Occ    1000 premium less agg AI.Occ_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson peel top-down                            note{two occurrence layers, highest first: marginal route, EX foots};
xpnl AI.OccUp  1000 premium less agg AI.OccUp_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson peel bottom-up                          note{the same tower lowest first: same total, reversed steps};
xpnl AI.Agg    1000 premium less agg AI.Agg_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 200 xs 600 deposit 50 and 400 xs 800 deposit 30 peel top-down                              note{aggregate layers only: per-atom, kappa ladder, every column foots};
xpnl AI.Mix    1000 premium less agg AI.Mix_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 cede 0.2 and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson aggregate net of 150 xs 300 deposit 25 peel top-down   note{both tiers plus a per-layer ceding commission leg};
xpnl AI.One    1000 premium less agg AI.One_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 500 xs 500 deposit 100 poisson aggregate net of 200 xs 400 deposit 40 peel top-down               note{one layer per tier: degenerates to the tier walk, per-atom};
xpnl AI.Label  1000 premium less agg AI.Label_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 as "Working layer" and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson peel bottom-up       note{a declared layer label names its step; undeclared falls back to the DecL descriptor};
xpnl AI.Share  1000 premium less agg AI.Share_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 50% po 200 xs 600 deposit 20 and 400 xs 800 deposit 15 peel top-down                    note{partial-share descriptor: the step reads "agg 50% po 200 xs 600"};
xpnl AI.Both   1000 premium less agg AI.Both_e 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 cede 0.2 and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson aggregate net of 100 xs 300 deposit 25 and 200 xs 400 deposit 15 peel top-down   note{two layers per tier: both All occurrence and All aggregate subtotal blocks}

# ============================================================
# SH. Sharpen: the (bs, log2) neighbourhood probe (1.0.0a192)
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_sharpen.py. bs_window / port_best_window CHOOSE a
# grid from the analytic moments before any FFT runs; sharpen AUDITS that choice
# afterwards, re-updating on the eight neighbouring cells (half / same / double
# bs by one step down / same / up in log2) and scoring each against the analytic
# moments in units of the validation tolerance. Good is a book whose auto-sized
# grid clears the target, so the probe gate skips it, and which fails badly when
# forced onto a starved grid, so the probe moves. Sym has a theoretically zero
# skew, the term the score must drop rather than validate against grid noise.
# Port exercises the portfolio route: the score reads the total only, and probe
# cells run add_exa=False. See dev/done/plan-sharpen.md.
agg SH.Good 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                               note{auto-sized grid clears the target; starved it fails and the probe walks back};
agg SH.Sym 1 claim dsev [-1 0 1] fixed                                                                            note{symmetric: analytic skew is exactly 0, so the skew term drops out of the score};
port SH.Port agg SH.A 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg SH.B 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson;
agg SH.Dice dfreq [3] dsev [1:6]                                                                                  note{exact discrete lattice: bs divides every atom, so sharpen pins the bucket and probes log2 only};
agg SH.Five 10 claims dsev [0 5 10 15] poisson                                                                    note{atoms on a 5-lattice: exact at bs=5, still exact but wasteful at bs=1};
port SH.PDisc agg SH.D1 dfreq [2] dsev [2 4 6] agg SH.D2 dfreq [2] dsev [4 8];
agg SH.Tower as "US Hurricane Reinsurance" 1.74 claims sev lognorm 8.501 cv 14.624 splice [0 500] poisson    note{cat XOL tower: bs=1/64 scores best but loses 2e-8 of its mass off the grid, so the soundness gate takes bs=1/32};
agg SH.Clean 100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1 poisson                                                                note{thin enough that the auto-sized grid holds every bit of mass: the probe gate skips it}


# EX. Exhibits module fixtures (1.0.0a200)
# ========================================
# Programs behind tests/test_exhibits.py and the canonical_dict snapshots
# captured by tests/capture_exhibit_snapshots.py ([Exhibits-Module] scaffold).
# Small exact discrete objects keep the committed IR snapshots cheap and
# deterministic; the labeled unit exercises the LabeledMixin relabel step in
# exhibit_frames (unit handle EX.UnitA serves as "Unit Alpha").
agg EX.Dice dfreq [3] dsev [1:6]                                                                                  note{summary/tail exhibit fixture; exact discrete lattice};
port EX.Port agg EX.UnitA as "Unit Alpha" 1 claim dsev [1 2 3] fixed agg EX.UnitB 2 claims dsev [2 4] fixed;
port EX.UnbPort note{unbounded pricing.allocate fixture: the mass family reaches the stat slices on the linear default} agg EX.UnbA 1 claim sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 fixed agg EX.UnbB 1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.8 fixed;
bv EX.BV dfreq [1 2] [.7 .3] dbvsev [[10 1 .4] [10 2 .1] [20 1 .2] [20 2 .1] [20 3 .2]]                           note{bivariate exhibit fixture: dependency + axis support blocks};
pnl EX.B 1000 premium less agg EX.Be 850 loss sev lognorm 100 cv 1 poisson                                        note{pnl exhibit fixture: stats/validation via the generic path};
dist EX.PH ph 0.5                                                                                                 note{distortion exhibit fixture: check-table emphasis path};
agg EX.Re dfreq [1 2] dsev [10 20 30] occurrence net of 10 xs 10                                                  note{reins exhibit fixture: layering block + per-stage summary};
port EX.RePort agg EX.ReA dfreq [1 2] dsev [10 20 30] occurrence net of 10 xs 10 agg EX.ReB 1 claim dsev [5 10] fixed;
xpnl EX.Tower 1000 prem less agg EX.TowerE 1000 prem at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence ceded to 500 xs 500 deposit 100 poisson                                        note{economic exhibit fixture: a two-step walk, so the ledger carries group/running-net/grand rows and a kappa ladder};
xpnl EX.Peel 1000 premium less agg EX.PeelE 1000 premium at 70% lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 60 and 300 xs 200 deposit 40 poisson peel top-down                     note{waterfall fixture: a stitched two-layer peel, so the ladder is marginal and the diversified column blanks}


# CH. Chart IR emitter fixtures (1.0.0a212)
# ========================================
# Programs behind tests/test_chart_severity.py ([Chart-Conversions]). One
# severity per branch the emitter has to get right: a heavy unbounded law
# where quantile spacing earns its keep, a bounded one whose quantiles repeat
# at the ceiling, and a discrete one with no density at all, which the
# emitter reads as probability mass rather than drawing a flat zero line.
# The reinsurance chart tests reuse the RR.* programs above rather than
# adding near-duplicates here.
sev CH.SevLN lognorm 100 cv 2                                                                                     note{chart fixture: unbounded heavy tail; quantile-spaced grid};
sev CH.SevB 500 * beta 2 3                                                                                        note{chart fixture: bounded; repeated quantiles at the ceiling};
sev CH.SevD dhistogram xps [1 10 40] [.5 .3 .2]                                                                   note{chart fixture: discrete; pdf is identically zero, so the panel reads mass};


# DP. Derived programs: the text that reproduces an object you arrived at
# ======================================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_derived_programs.py ([Derived-Programs]). Both
# halves belong here: the inputs the tests build, and the DecL the three
# members hand back, since the whole point of them is producing text. The
# outputs are shown terse and are what the members actually returned; the two
# computed premiums drift with the FFT and are mirrored for shape, not value.
# Note the two reinsurance slots, which sit on opposite sides of the frequency
# clause, and the trailer clauses, which are merged rather than repeated: one
# note and one hints per statement, however many times the object is derived
# from. See dev/done/plan-derived-programs.md.

# the inputs
agg DP.Moves 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                              note{derived-programs input: the probe improves this grid, so sharpen_program pins it};
agg DP.Clean 100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1 poisson                                                                note{thin enough that the grid is confirmed: a note, and deliberately no hints};
agg DP.Noted 100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1 poisson note{a stored note};
agg DP.Hinted 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson hints{bs=1/32; padding=2};
agg DP.Both 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson note{a stored note} hints{bs=1/32; padding=2};
agg DP.Premium 1000 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                               note{states its own premium, so the P&L inherits rather than sizing one};
agg DP.OddPremium 1000.125 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                        note{an inherited premium is never re-rounded: only a sized one is};
agg DP.Ceded 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 250 xs 250 poisson aggregate net of 9000 xs 1000;
agg DP.Discrete dfreq [1 2 3] dsev [10 20 30];
agg DP.Approx 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson approximate sgamma                                          note{occurrence cession refused here: the fit bypasses the per-occurrence convolution};
agg DP.Labeled as "Motor book" 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                            note{the label rides down to the wrapping P&L as its loss-leg name};
port DP.Port note{a stored note} agg DP.PortA 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg DP.PortB 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson;
pnl DP.Wrapped 1000 premium less agg DP.WrappedE 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                          note{already a P&L: pnl_program refuses to wrap it twice};

# [PnL-Reinsurance-Pricing] (1.0.0a306): engines whose cessions pnl_program
# prices when handed a net_combined_ratio. Tower is the plan's worked example,
# both tiers at full placement; Half and Full are the same layer at two
# placements, for the share invariance; Two carries two layers, for the
# per-layer ratio sequence. The last three arrive already priced, one on each
# of the three bases, and Rate is the one the ladder refuses: a rate resolves
# against the very premium the ladder is computing.
agg DP.Tower 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson aggregate net of 500 xs 1000;
agg DP.HalfPlaced 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 50% po 100 xs 100 poisson;
agg DP.FullPlaced 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson;
agg DP.Two 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 and 1000 xs 1000 poisson;
agg DP.Deposit 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 200 and 1000 xs 1000 poisson;
agg DP.Rol 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rol 1.5 and 1000 xs 1000 poisson;
agg DP.Rate 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 rate 0.13 and 1000 xs 1000 poisson;

# sharpen_program: the grid moved, the grid was confirmed, and the two merges
agg DP.Moves 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson hints{log2=16; bs=8};
agg DP.Clean 100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1 poisson note{sharpen: grid confirmed, no change};
agg DP.Noted 100 claims sev gamma 100 cv 1 poisson note{a stored note; sharpen: grid confirmed, no change};
agg DP.Hinted 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson hints{bs=1/2; padding=2; log2=17};
port DP.Port note{a stored note} hints{log2=15; bs=20} agg DP.PortA 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg DP.PortB 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson;

# pnl_program: an inherited premium, a sized one, the loss-leg label, and the
# INLINE PORTFOLIO engine (1.0.0a216). Both engine kinds write their body out,
# so the text needs no loaded referent and round-trips here in isolation, which
# the `port.NAME` reference form it replaced could not.
pnl DP.Premium_PnL inherit premium less agg DP.Premium 1000 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.OddPremium_PnL inherit premium less agg DP.OddPremium 1000.125 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.Discrete_PnL 80 premium less agg DP.Discrete dfreq [1 2 3] dsev [10 20 30] less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.Labeled_PnL 14286 premium less agg DP.Labeled as "Motor book" 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.Port_PnL 11239 premium less port DP.Port agg DP.PortA 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg DP.PortB 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.InlineLabeled as "Book P&L" 1500 premium less port DP.InlineE as "Motor book" agg DP.IA 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg DP.IB 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson;

# pnl_program with net_combined_ratio, the combined-ratio ladder
# ([PnL-Reinsurance-Pricing], 1.0.0a306): the net book and every cover are
# priced separately and added, then grossed up once for expenses, so the
# wrapping P&L states a ceded premium and builds without the zero-premium
# warning. Each cover is written as a deposit, the one premium form that does
# not reference the premium being computed. Deposits are quoted at 100 percent
# placement and scaled by the share at resolution, which is why DP.HalfPlaced
# writes the same 196 as DP.FullPlaced and pays half of it. DP.Two is from a
# per-layer sequence, 0.80 on the working layer and 0.60 on the excess one;
# DP.Deposit keeps the 200 its author wrote and the ladder prices only the
# layer that had no clause. The premiums drift with the FFT and are mirrored
# for shape, not value.
pnl DP.Tower_PnL 1481 premium less agg DP.Tower 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 196 poisson aggregate net of 500 xs 1000 deposit 90.01 less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.HalfPlaced_PnL 1481 premium less agg DP.HalfPlaced 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 50% po 100 xs 100 deposit 196 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.Two_PnL 1538 premium less agg DP.Two 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 221 and 1000 xs 1000 deposit 54.34 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;
pnl DP.Deposit_PnL 1486 premium less agg DP.Deposit 10 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 deposit 200 and 1000 xs 1000 deposit 36.23 poisson less 0.25 premium expenses;

# reins_program: the occurrence slot, the aggregate slot, both at once, and a
# cession that replaces its own tier and leaves the other alone
agg DP.Moves 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 500 xs 500 poisson;
agg DP.Moves 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson aggregate net of 9000 xs 1000;
agg DP.Moves 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 500 xs 500 poisson aggregate net of 9000 xs 1000;
agg DP.Ceded 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of 500 xs 500 poisson aggregate net of 9000 xs 1000;
agg DP.Discrete dfreq [1 2 3] dsev [10 20 30] occurrence net of 15 xs 5


# WP. Warning-policy fixtures (1.0.0a219)
# =======================================
# Programs behind tests/test_warning_policy.py ([Warning-Policy]). Defective is
# an unlimited Lomax with alpha 1.5, so no grid of finite extent holds it and
# the deficit is set by bs alone: coarse enough and it clears the materiality
# floor, which is what makes it warn. Clean is thin enough that what its grid
# loses is immaterial, so it must stay silent. Tower is the sharpen fixture
# reused: the probe counts a warning per cell, which once-per-session must not
# blind. See dev/done/plan-warning-policy.md.
agg WP.Defective 1 claim sev 0.5 * lomax 1.5 fixed                                                                note{unlimited infinite-variance Lomax: a material pmf deficit at any affordable grid};
agg WP.Clean 100 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                              note{immaterial deficit: the floor exists so this one says nothing};
port WP.Port agg WP.PA 50 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson agg WP.PB 20 claims sev lognorm 200 cv 1 poisson;
agg WP.Tower as "US Hurricane Reinsurance" 1.74 claims sev lognorm 8.501 cv 14.624 splice [0 500] poisson    note{probe fixture: warn_once_isolated keeps every cell's warning countable}


# PT. Type-I Pareto fixtures (1.0.0a221)
# ======================================
# Program behind tests/test_partial_e_pareto.py. `sev {xm} * pareto {alpha}` is
# the single-parameter (Type-I) form, support [xm, inf), which scipy spells
# scale=xm, loc=0. It had no analytic partial-moment branch, so every build
# quadratured a heavy tail to infinity and logged about it; the closed form
# makes the mean exactly alpha*xm/(alpha-1). Compare Liu2026 in the
# reproductions, which is entirely Type-I Pareto.
agg PT.TypeOne 1 claim sev 1 * pareto 2.5 fixed                                                                   note{Type-I Pareto: analytic partial moments, mean alpha*xm/(alpha-1) = 5/3}


# RV. Reinsurance pricing views (1.0.0a223)
# =========================================
# Programs behind tests/test_reins_view_pricing.py ([Loss-Lab-Round-3] phase
# A). The `reins_view=` keyword names which of a cession's distributions to
# price on, so the fixtures are the three shapes a program can take: one stage,
# both stages, and a `ceded to` cession whose own density is its CEDED view
# rather than its net. The last is the point of the phase: an object holds one
# of its distributions and which one depends on how the program was written,
# so a caller reading `density_df` and calling it "net" is right half the time.
# The portfolio pairs a ceding unit with a clean one, since a book's own total
# already IS its net view and only `gross` says something new there.
agg RV.Occ 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson                                     note{one stage: reins_views is gross, ceded, net};
agg RV.Both 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson aggregate net of 1000 xs 4000      note{two stages: adds ceded occ and net occ};
agg RV.Ceded 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 2 occurrence ceded to 100 xs 100 poisson                                 note{own density is the CEDED view, not the net};
agg RV.Clean 30 claims sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 poisson                                                               note{no cession: reins_views is empty and reins_view= is refused};
port RV.Book agg RV.Occ 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 2 occurrence net of 100 xs 100 poisson agg RV.Clean 30 claims sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 poisson

# ============================================================
# RS. Reflected severity as an ordinary loss (1.0.0a230)
# ============================================================
# A reflected body (`shift - X`, or the bare `-X`) under plain `sev` is built
# like any other severity whose support reaches below zero (`10 * norm + 5`,
# `lognorm 5 cv 1 - 10`): the layered-loss transform clamps x<0 -> 0, the
# severity stays non-signed, and layers and occurrence reinsurance work. It was
# rejected outright before 1.0.0a230, which forced `ssev` and with it the whole
# signed code path even on a bounded reflection that never goes negative.
# `ssev` keeps the negative support and is unchanged. A `sev` reflection that
# does reach below zero warns (ReflectedSeverityClampWarning) naming the clamped
# mass; in the limit everything clamps and the severity is a point mass at 0.
# The keyword follows sev_signed alone under the unparser, so these round-trip
# as `sev`, not `ssev`. See tests/test_negative_x.py and
# dev/done/plan-reflected-loss-severity.md.
agg RS.Bounded  10 claims sev 10 - lognorm 1.5 splice [0 10] poisson                                                note{splice bounds the reflection to [0, 10]: an ordinary loss, nothing clamps};
agg RS.Clamped  10 claims sev 100 - lognorm 80 cv .2 poisson                                                        note{premium minus loss read as a loss: the negative tail clamps at 0 and warns};
agg RS.Negate   10 claims sev -lognorm 10 cv 0.5 poisson                                                            note{fully reflected: everything clamps, severity is a point mass at 0};
agg RS.Shift    10 claims sev -lognorm 2 cv 0.5 + 5 poisson                                                         note{tight bind (-X) + 5 == 5 - X, clamped at 0};
agg RS.Splice   10 claims ssev lognorm 1.5 splice [0 10] poisson                                                    note{signed and spliced: raw moments follow the splice (fixed 1.0.0a230)}

# ============================================================
# PR. Pricing result objects (1.0.0a259)
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_pricing_results.py ([Pricing-Result-Objects]).
# `calibrate_distortions` and `evaluate` return a result rather than a frame,
# and a result carries the allocation of its target: across units for a book,
# across views for a cession, and nowhere at all for a single distribution with
# no cession, which is why the three shapes are all here. PR.Priced declares a
# premium so `evaluate()` has a consideration to measure against without one
# being passed.
agg PR.Clean  250 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 poisson                                                             note{one distribution: the allocation story is the single calibration row};
agg PR.Re     250 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 276 xs 55 poisson                                 note{a cession: the target spreads over the views};
agg PR.Priced 1000 premium at 0.7 lr sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 poisson                                                   note{a declared premium, so evaluate() needs no argument};
port PR.Book agg PR.UnitA as "Unit Alpha" 1 claim sev gamma 100 cv 0.5 fixed agg PR.UnitB 1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.8 fixed

# ============================================================
# UA. The p = 1 asset anchor guard (1.0.0a260)
# ============================================================
# Programs behind tests/test_unbounded_anchor.py ([Unbounded-Anchor-Guard]).
# The pair that matters is UA.Book against UA.Dice: a Poisson count has no
# ceiling, so p=1 on it resolves to the top of the FFT grid and moves with
# log2, while a dfreq/dsev law has a genuine maximum and p=1 means it.
# UA.Limited is the trap the guard is really about: a bounded per-occurrence
# layer under an unbounded count is still unbounded. UA.Port is the worst-of
# rule, one Poisson unit beside a bounded one.
agg UA.Book    250 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 poisson                                                            note{unbounded: p=1 is refused};
agg UA.Limited 250 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 900 xs 100 poisson                               note{a bounded layer under an unbounded count is still unbounded};
agg UA.Dice    dfreq [3] dsev [1:6]                                                                                 note{bounded: p=1 is the maximum loss and means it};
port UA.Port agg UA.PU1 250 claims sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 poisson agg UA.PU2 dfreq [2] dsev [1 2 3]

# ============================================================
# EA. The evaluation asset anchor (1.0.0a261)
# ============================================================
# The author's reference programs for the round trip, 2026-08-12: calibrate at
# coc 0.15 and p 0.99, take the implied premium at the resolved asset level,
# evaluate that premium at the same anchor, recover the calibrated parameters.
# See tests/test_evaluate_anchor.py ([Evaluate-Asset-Anchor]). The trio is one
# book, the same book reinsured, and a two unit portfolio, so the round trip is
# asserted on the three shapes the Pricing pane serves.
agg EA.BasicBook   250 claims 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 poisson                                              note{the reference book: a per occurrence limit under an unbounded count};
agg EA.BasicBookRe 250 claims 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5 occurrence net of 276 xs 55 poisson                  note{the same book reinsured};
port EA.Basic agg EA.BasicA dfreq [1] sev gamma 100 cv 1 agg EA.BasicB dfreq [1] sev gamma 50 cv 0.8

# ============================================================
# FYI. Informational premium on the claims / loss heads (1.0.0a266)
# ============================================================
# [FYI-Premium-Exposure-Head]: `<count> claims <P> premium` and `<EL> loss
# <P> premium` carry a booked premium alongside the sizing head. The head
# sizes the law exactly as before; the premium is informational only: it
# books exp_premium, the loss ratio back-fills from the realized expected
# loss, and `pnl ... inherit premium` reads it. No `and` joins the clause
# (comma is whitespace, so the comma spelling below is the same program).
# FYI.Layered pins the disambiguation that matters: after the suffix, a
# layers clause must still parse (premium vs xs anchors). FYI.Engine pins
# the pnl interplay: the engine's FYI premium feeds `inherit premium`, and
# the `premium less` divider is untouched. See tests/test_fyi_premium.py.
agg FYI.ClaimsPrem 5 claims 20000 premium sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                              note{claims head with an informational premium};
agg FYI.LossPrem 500 loss 650 premium sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                  note{loss head with an informational premium};
agg FYI.Comma 5 claims, 20000 premium sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                  note{comma-as-whitespace spelling};
agg FYI.Labeled 5 claims as counts 20000 premium as booked sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                             note{both the head and the premium carry as labels};
agg FYI.Quoted 500 loss 650 premium as "Booked Premium" sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                note{quoted premium label};
agg FYI.Layered 5 claims 20000 premium 1000 xs 100 sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                     note{a layer after the suffix: xs vs premium disambiguates};
pnl FYI.Engine inherit premium less agg FYI.EngineE 5 claims 20000 premium sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson             note{inherit premium reads the engine FYI premium}

# ============================================================
# PA. Full arithmetic inside parentheses (1.0.0a268)
# ============================================================
# [DecL-Paren-Arithmetic]: `+`, `-` and `*` join `/`, `**`, `^` and `exp`,
# legal only inside parentheses. The paren gate is load bearing: those three
# operators already mean something in severity scaling and shifting and in the
# builtin_agg algebra, and NUMBER absorbs a glued leading minus, so admitting
# them into a bare expression would make `2 * 3 * agg.X` and `[1 -2]` ambiguous.
# A paren holds exactly one expression, never a list, so `(1-.25)` has
# subtraction as its only reading. Everything evaluates at parse time, so the
# spec carries plain floats and the canonical text shows the evaluated literal:
# PA.Head decompiles as `10 claims 133333.33333333334 premium`. The lines below
# put paren math in each slot that takes a number. See
# tests/test_paren_arithmetic.py.
agg PA.Head (4 + 3*2) claims (100_000/(1-.25)) premium 1000 xs 0 sev (exp(-1 * .4**2/2)) * lognorm .4 poisson       note{the target program: computed count, computed FYI premium, computed scale};
agg PA.Scale 10 claims sev (exp(-1 * .4**2/2)) * lognorm .4 poisson                                                 note{the lognormal mean normalizer exp(-sigma^2/2) written inline};
agg PA.Layer 5 claims (500*2) xs (0+0) sev lognorm 100 cv 2 poisson                                                 note{a computed layer and attachment};
agg PA.Freq 10 claims dsev [1] binomial (0.2 + 0.3)                                                                 note{a computed frequency parameter};
agg PA.Range dfreq [(1+0):(3*2)] dsev [1]                                                                           note{a computed range: the six dice outcomes};
agg PA.Reins 5 claims 1000 xs 0 sev lognorm 100 cv 2 occurrence net of (100*2) xs (50 + 50) poisson                  note{a computed reinsurance layer};
agg PA.Vector dfreq [1] dsev [1 -2]                                                                                 note{brackets are unchanged: a glued minus keeps the two element list}

# ============================================================
# DRV. Derived premium (1.0.0a270)
# ============================================================
# [Derived-Premium]: `derive premium` reads the engine's technical premium T
# as risk loaded and grosses it up for the `less` clause expenses, P = (T +
# fixed total) / (1 - premium ratio total), so premium net of expenses returns
# exactly T. Fixed and premium bases only: a loss basis expense, an engine
# without premium, and premium ratios totalling one or more are build errors.
# An expense free head is legal and equals `inherit premium`. See
# tests/test_pnl_derive_premium.py and dev/plan-derived-premium.md.
pnl DRV.Worked derive premium less agg DRV.WorkedE 100 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 50 cv 0.8 poisson less 10 fixed expense as "App Fee" 2 fixed expense as "Admin fee" 5% premium expense as "TLF" 15% premium expense as "Commission" 7% premium expense as "G&A"   note{the worked example: P = (100 + 12) / (1 - 0.27)};
pnl DRV.NoExp derive premium less agg DRV.NoExpE 100 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 50 cv 0.8 poisson                                     note{no expense clause: the derivation is the identity, P = T};
pnl DRV.Multi derive premium as "Booked" less agg DRV.MultiE 100 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 50 cv 0.8 poisson less 5% premium expense and 20% premium expense and 12 fixed expense   note{and joined terms accumulate, combined ratio style, with a labeled head};
pnl DRV.Port derive premium less port DRV.PortB agg DRV.U1 50 premium at 0.5 lr sev lognorm 20 cv 0.6 poisson agg DRV.U2 50 premium at 0.5 lr sev lognorm 30 cv 0.9 poisson less 20% premium expenses   note{the accumulated portfolio premium grosses up, parallel to inherit};
xpnl DRV.Walk derive premium less agg DRV.WalkE 100 premium at 0.65 lr sev lognorm 50 cv 0.8 occurrence net of 30 xs 30 deposit 5 poisson less 25% premium expenses   note{the walk shares the premium head, so it derives too}

# ============================================================
# ASV. An agg or port as a severity (1.0.0a291)
# ============================================================
# [Agg-As-Severity]: `sev agg.NAME` / `sev port.NAME`, with the `ssev` and `!`
# forms. DecL's one DEFERRED reference: the severity is the referenced object's
# computed output, so the parse records the name and the underwriter resolves it
# at build time. A referenced declaration must pin its own grid with
# hints{log2=...; bs=...}; `with_hints` writes that clause for you. The two `!`s
# are different: the frequency one holds the zt/zm mean, the severity one keeps
# the layer unconditional. See tests/test_agg_as_severity.py and
# dev/done/plan-agg-port-as-sev.md.
agg ASV.Inner 1.5 claims 100 xs 0 sev gamma 50 cv 2 poisson zt hints{log2=16; bs=1/32}                            note{the certified inner: per-policy law, at least one claim, 100 per claimant};
agg ASV.Book 5000 claims 300 xs 0 sev agg.ASV.Inner mixed gamma .2                                                  note{the split limit: 5000 policies, 300 policy aggregate as an occurrence limit; the layers clause conditions by default};
agg ASV.BookUncond 5000 claims 300 xs 0 sev agg.ASV.Inner ! mixed gamma .2                                          note{the same book with the layer kept unconditional by the severity !};
agg ASV.NoLayer 10 claims sev agg.ASV.Inner poisson                                                                 note{no layers clause, so the reference is convolved whole};
agg ASV.Dice dfreq [1] dsev [1 2 3] hints{log2=8; bs=1}                                                             note{a discrete inner: the outer is then an exact self convolution};
agg ASV.TwoDice dfreq [2] sev agg.ASV.Dice                                                                          note{two of the inner, exactly};
agg ASV.Chain dfreq [2] sev agg.ASV.TwoDice hints{log2=8; bs=1}                                                     note{depth 2: a reference whose own severity is a reference};
port ASV.Port hints{log2=16; bs=1/32} agg ASV.PortUnit 1.5 claims 100 xs 0 sev gamma 50 cv 2 poisson zt;
agg ASV.FromPort 10 claims sev port.ASV.Port poisson                                                                note{a portfolio total as a severity};
agg ASV.SignedInner dfreq [1] ssev 100 - lognorm 50 cv .5 hints{log2=12; bs=1/16}                                   note{a signed source};
agg ASV.UsesSigned dfreq [1] ssev agg.ASV.SignedInner                                                               note{ssev over a signed reference keeps the negative atoms}

# ============================================================
# MXT. Mixture component thinning (1.0.0a316)
# ============================================================
# [Mixture-Thinning-Moments]: a severity mixture splits the claim count, and the
# component count is Binomial(N, w) whatever N is. The weight cannot ride the
# count, because only a Poisson thinning is recoverable from its mean. These
# pin the families where the two readings differ: fixed and dfreq (a degenerate
# count thins to a binomial, not to a smaller degenerate count), binomial (the
# trial count is fixed and the success probability thins), the Poisson clusters,
# and logarithmic, whose mean is bounded below so the old route could not even
# express a small component. MXT.Dfreq and MXT.Fixed are the same frequency
# written two ways and must agree throughout stats_df. See
# tests/test_mixture_thinning.py and dev/done/plan-mixture-thinning-moments.md.
agg MXT.Dfreq dfreq [3] sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1]                                                            note{a one atom dfreq is a fixed count; must match MXT.Fixed exactly};
agg MXT.Fixed 3 claims sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1] fixed                                                       note{the same frequency written with an exposure clause};
agg MXT.DfreqSpread dfreq [1 2 6] sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1]                                                  note{a genuinely spread empirical count, which has no fixed equivalent};
agg MXT.Binomial 3 claims sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1] binomial 0.5                                             note{Bin(r, p) thins to Bin(r, wp): the trials do not thin};
agg MXT.Logarithmic 3 claims sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1] logarithmic                                           note{the 0.1 component has thinned mean 0.3, below the logarithmic minimum};
agg MXT.NeymanA 3 claims sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1] neymana 2                                                 note{a Poisson cluster count, negatively dependent components};
agg MXT.Pascal 3 claims sev [1 10] * expon wts [0.9 0.1] pascal 0.5 2                                               note{a Poisson stopped sum of negative binomials};
agg MXT.MixedExponential dfreq [1] sev [2764 24548 275654 1917469 10000000] * expon wts [0.824796 0.159065 0.014444 0.001624 7.1e-05] hints{log2=18; bs=500}   note{ISO/Verisk style commercial auto mixed exponential, mean 13,990; the case that surfaced the defect};
